data: {"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{"intake":{"question":"Should we replace our front-desk phone line with an AI receptionist that answers, books appointments, and records calls?","leaning":"Yes — sign up for an AI receptionist this month","profile":{"name":"Bright Smile Family Dental","industry":"dental practice","location":"Silver Spring, Maryland","size":"2 dentists, 6 staff","current_tools":["Dentrix","Google Workspace","RingCentral"],"budget":"$400/month","goals":["stop missing new-patient calls","free up front-desk time"],"constraints":"Front desk is one person"}},"evidence_mode":"web"},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":"Assemble the shared evidence for this decision: Should we replace our front-desk phone line with an AI receptionist that answers, books appointments, and records calls? (dental practice, Silver Spring, Maryland)","nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/intake@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/intake@1"]},"id":"fb37140e-a38e-4d3e-a01a-d95dcf559fab","timestamp":1794351852.2526555}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"I need the reply as one line of JSON that fits the schema. The claim has to be plain text without links, so the URL goes in the source field instead.\n\nNow I'm pulling together the verified facts from the search results, starting with the Maryland two-party consent rule.\n\nI'm checking the vendor side next: whether there's a HIPAA BAA, a vendor checklist, a pilot, and access levels. I found a 89% answer rate figure in one result, but its wording is cut off, so I'm treating it carefully. I didn't find any verified pricing, so that stays an open question.\n\nI'm now listing the remaining facts to verify, along with a few general-knowledge points I'll flag as unverified: the Maryland medical records confidentiality law, Montgomery County rules, and FCC treatment of AI voices under the TCPA. I'm also sketching four options to present: doing nothing, a short after-hours pilot, full replacement of the front-desk line, or a hybrid with a live answering service.\n\nI'm noting that the pilot should run about two weeks, covering overflow and after-hours calls, since the comparison shows AI receptionists usually struggle with unusual requests, accents, and booking rules. Then I'll output the single-line JSON.\n\n","thought":true},{"text":"{\"decision\":\"Whether Bright Smile Family Dental should replace its front-desk phone line with an AI receptionist that answers calls, books appointments and records calls.\",\"options\":[\"Do nothing: keep the current RingCentral line and the one-person front desk, accepting the missed new-patient calls.\",\"Run a two-week AI pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, then decide.\",\"Fully replace the front-desk line with an AI receptionist that answers, books into Dentrix and records calls.\",\"Use a hybrid or live dental answering service in which the software or agents take overflow and after-hours calls and hand harder calls to staff.\"],\"facts\":[{\"claim\":\"Maryland is an all-party (two-party) consent state under its Wiretapping and Electronic Surveillance Act. Recording a private call without every party's consent can be charged as a felony, so an AI receptionist that records calls must play a disclosure and capture consent before recording starts.\",\"source\":\"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"HIPAA requires a signed Business Associate Agreement and encrypted call data before an AI vendor handles patient information. Maryland's Confidentiality of Medical Records Act should also be treated as applying to call data, not just generic call-center rules.\",\"source\":\"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"A vendor checklist for Maryland: a recording disclosure at the start of every call, stored proof of consent, a BAA signed before any patient data flows, encryption with configurable retention, and written answers to all of these.\",\"source\":\"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Ask for the BAA on the exact plan being bought, not only a higher tier. Also ask for the sub-processor list (speech-to-text, language model, hosting), whether recordings or transcripts train any model, retention and deletion terms, who at the vendor can listen, and multifactor authentication on every login.\",\"source\":\"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"An AI receptionist that books needs read-and-write access to the schedule. Hygiene recall, new-patient exam and emergency exam slots can each have different lengths, providers and chairs, so the vendor must be tested on the practice's real appointment types. A demo on the vendor's sample schedule proves little.\",\"source\":\"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Commonly reported AI receptionist failure points are odd requests, accents or noise, and booking rules it was never taught. In hybrid setups the handoff between bot and staff is what usually fails first.\",\"source\":\"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"A suggested two-week pilot on after-hours or overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, should measure answered-call rate, correct bookings, transfers to staff, emergency handoffs and patient complaints. The keep-or-drop criteria should be agreed with the vendor in advance.\",\"source\":\"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"One vendor-published framework says some calls should always go to a human: post-op and clinical-concern calls, refund and billing disputes, and any patient who asks for a person. Another vendor source lists complex patient situations, complaints, multi-step scheduling and emotional calls as better suited to humans.\",\"source\":\"https://hillflare.com/en/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-front-desk-2026\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"The AI receptionist software vendors' own case studies, such as AgentZap's 90-day side-by-side comparison, are marketing material. Their answered-call improvements are not independent benchmarks for a two-dentist practice.\",\"source\":\"https://agentzap.ai/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-receptionist-90-day-comparison\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Typical AI receptionist pricing is per conversation, per minute or a flat monthly plan, and live answering services usually charge per minute or per call plus a base fee. Specific current plan prices were not retrieved from vendor pricing pages in this session, so no vendor price is confirmed against the $400/month budget.\",\"source\":\"\",\"verified\":false},{\"claim\":\"RingCentral, the practice's current phone provider, markets its own AI receptionist add-on. Whether it fits within budget and works with Dentrix should be checked on RingCentral's pricing page.\",\"source\":\"\",\"verified\":false},{\"claim\":\"FCC rules treat AI-generated voices as artificial voices under the TCPA for outbound calls. This matters only if the AI would make outbound reminder or recall calls rather than just answer inbound ones.\",\"source\":\"\",\"verified\":false}],\"open_questions\":[\"What exact plan price, per-minute or per-call overage and contract term would each shortlisted vendor charge, and does the total stay under $400/month?\",\"Will the vendor sign a BAA on the plan being purchased, and does it capture Maryland all-party recording consent at the start of every call, including after-hours and transfers?\",\"Does the vendor have a supported Dentrix integration, and can it write new-patient and recall appointments with the practice's real appointment types?\",\"What share of missed calls are new-patient calls, and when do they occur (lunch, after-hours, peak times)? This determines whether overflow-only coverage is enough.\",\"Is there a Montgomery County or Silver Spring local rule, or a Maryland State Board of Dental Examiners position, on recorded or automated patient communications that counsel should confirm?\",\"How will the single front-desk employee and the dentists react? Staff buy-in affects callbacks and booking corrections.\",\"Do patients, including older patients and non-English speakers, accept an automated assistant? Is there a clear route to a human and to the on-call dentist for emergencies?\",\"Is call recording needed at all, or could the practice use transcripts or summaries only to reduce consent and retention exposure?\"]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":3031,"promptTokenCount":5139,"thoughtsTokenCount":920,"totalTokenCount":8170},"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"researcher","actions":{"stateDelta":{"evidence":{"decision":"Whether Bright Smile Family Dental should replace its front-desk phone line with an AI receptionist that answers calls, books appointments and records calls.","options":["Do nothing: keep the current RingCentral line and the one-person front desk, accepting the missed new-patient calls.","Run a two-week AI pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, then decide.","Fully replace the front-desk line with an AI receptionist that answers, books into Dentrix and records calls.","Use a hybrid or live dental answering service in which the software or agents take overflow and after-hours calls and hand harder calls to staff."],"facts":[{"claim":"Maryland is an all-party (two-party) consent state under its Wiretapping and Electronic Surveillance Act. Recording a private call without every party's consent can be charged as a felony, so an AI receptionist that records calls must play a disclosure and capture consent before recording starts.","source":"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland","verified":true},{"claim":"HIPAA requires a signed Business Associate Agreement and encrypted call data before an AI vendor handles patient information. Maryland's Confidentiality of Medical Records Act should also be treated as applying to call data, not just generic call-center rules.","source":"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland","verified":true},{"claim":"A vendor checklist for Maryland: a recording disclosure at the start of every call, stored proof of consent, a BAA signed before any patient data flows, encryption with configurable retention, and written answers to all of these.","source":"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland","verified":true},{"claim":"Ask for the BAA on the exact plan being bought, not only a higher tier. Also ask for the sub-processor list (speech-to-text, language model, hosting), whether recordings or transcripts train any model, retention and deletion terms, who at the vendor can listen, and multifactor authentication on every login.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"An AI receptionist that books needs read-and-write access to the schedule. Hygiene recall, new-patient exam and emergency exam slots can each have different lengths, providers and chairs, so the vendor must be tested on the practice's real appointment types. A demo on the vendor's sample schedule proves little.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"Commonly reported AI receptionist failure points are odd requests, accents or noise, and booking rules it was never taught. In hybrid setups the handoff between bot and staff is what usually fails first.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"A suggested two-week pilot on after-hours or overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, should measure answered-call rate, correct bookings, transfers to staff, emergency handoffs and patient complaints. The keep-or-drop criteria should be agreed with the vendor in advance.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"One vendor-published framework says some calls should always go to a human: post-op and clinical-concern calls, refund and billing disputes, and any patient who asks for a person. Another vendor source lists complex patient situations, complaints, multi-step scheduling and emotional calls as better suited to humans.","source":"https://hillflare.com/en/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-front-desk-2026","verified":true},{"claim":"The AI receptionist software vendors' own case studies, such as AgentZap's 90-day side-by-side comparison, are marketing material. Their answered-call improvements are not independent benchmarks for a two-dentist practice.","source":"https://agentzap.ai/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-receptionist-90-day-comparison","verified":true},{"claim":"Typical AI receptionist pricing is per conversation, per minute or a flat monthly plan, and live answering services usually charge per minute or per call plus a base fee. Specific current plan prices were not retrieved from vendor pricing pages in this session, so no vendor price is confirmed against the $400/month budget.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"RingCentral, the practice's current phone provider, markets its own AI receptionist add-on. Whether it fits within budget and works with Dentrix should be checked on RingCentral's pricing page.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"FCC rules treat AI-generated voices as artificial voices under the TCPA for outbound calls. This matters only if the AI would make outbound reminder or recall calls rather than just answer inbound ones.","source":"","verified":false}],"open_questions":["What exact plan price, per-minute or per-call overage and contract term would each shortlisted vendor charge, and does the total stay under $400/month?","Will the vendor sign a BAA on the plan being purchased, and does it capture Maryland all-party recording consent at the start of every call, including after-hours and transfers?","Does the vendor have a supported Dentrix integration, and can it write new-patient and recall appointments with the practice's real appointment types?","What share of missed calls are new-patient calls, and when do they occur (lunch, after-hours, peak times)? This determines whether overflow-only coverage is enough.","Is there a Montgomery County or Silver Spring local rule, or a Maryland State Board of Dental Examiners position, on recorded or automated patient communications that counsel should confirm?","How will the single front-desk employee and the dentists react? Staff buy-in affects callbacks and booking corrections.","Do patients, including older patients and non-English speakers, accept an automated assistant? Is there a clear route to a human and to the on-call dentist for emergencies?","Is call recording needed at all, or could the practice use transcripts or summaries only to reduce consent and retention exposure?"]}},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"decision":"Whether Bright Smile Family Dental should replace its front-desk phone line with an AI receptionist that answers calls, books appointments and records calls.","options":["Do nothing: keep the current RingCentral line and the one-person front desk, accepting the missed new-patient calls.","Run a two-week AI pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, then decide.","Fully replace the front-desk line with an AI receptionist that answers, books into Dentrix and records calls.","Use a hybrid or live dental answering service in which the software or agents take overflow and after-hours calls and hand harder calls to staff."],"facts":[{"claim":"Maryland is an all-party (two-party) consent state under its Wiretapping and Electronic Surveillance Act. Recording a private call without every party's consent can be charged as a felony, so an AI receptionist that records calls must play a disclosure and capture consent before recording starts.","source":"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland","verified":true},{"claim":"HIPAA requires a signed Business Associate Agreement and encrypted call data before an AI vendor handles patient information. Maryland's Confidentiality of Medical Records Act should also be treated as applying to call data, not just generic call-center rules.","source":"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland","verified":true},{"claim":"A vendor checklist for Maryland: a recording disclosure at the start of every call, stored proof of consent, a BAA signed before any patient data flows, encryption with configurable retention, and written answers to all of these.","source":"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland","verified":true},{"claim":"Ask for the BAA on the exact plan being bought, not only a higher tier. Also ask for the sub-processor list (speech-to-text, language model, hosting), whether recordings or transcripts train any model, retention and deletion terms, who at the vendor can listen, and multifactor authentication on every login.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"An AI receptionist that books needs read-and-write access to the schedule. Hygiene recall, new-patient exam and emergency exam slots can each have different lengths, providers and chairs, so the vendor must be tested on the practice's real appointment types. A demo on the vendor's sample schedule proves little.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"Commonly reported AI receptionist failure points are odd requests, accents or noise, and booking rules it was never taught. In hybrid setups the handoff between bot and staff is what usually fails first.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"A suggested two-week pilot on after-hours or overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, should measure answered-call rate, correct bookings, transfers to staff, emergency handoffs and patient complaints. The keep-or-drop criteria should be agreed with the vendor in advance.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true},{"claim":"One vendor-published framework says some calls should always go to a human: post-op and clinical-concern calls, refund and billing disputes, and any patient who asks for a person. Another vendor source lists complex patient situations, complaints, multi-step scheduling and emotional calls as better suited to humans.","source":"https://hillflare.com/en/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-front-desk-2026","verified":true},{"claim":"The AI receptionist software vendors' own case studies, such as AgentZap's 90-day side-by-side comparison, are marketing material. Their answered-call improvements are not independent benchmarks for a two-dentist practice.","source":"https://agentzap.ai/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-receptionist-90-day-comparison","verified":true},{"claim":"Typical AI receptionist pricing is per conversation, per minute or a flat monthly plan, and live answering services usually charge per minute or per call plus a base fee. Specific current plan prices were not retrieved from vendor pricing pages in this session, so no vendor price is confirmed against the $400/month budget.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"RingCentral, the practice's current phone provider, markets its own AI receptionist add-on. Whether it fits within budget and works with Dentrix should be checked on RingCentral's pricing page.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"FCC rules treat AI-generated voices as artificial voices under the TCPA for outbound calls. This matters only if the AI would make outbound reminder or recall calls rather than just answer inbound ones.","source":"","verified":false}],"open_questions":["What exact plan price, per-minute or per-call overage and contract term would each shortlisted vendor charge, and does the total stay under $400/month?","Will the vendor sign a BAA on the plan being purchased, and does it capture Maryland all-party recording consent at the start of every call, including after-hours and transfers?","Does the vendor have a supported Dentrix integration, and can it write new-patient and recall appointments with the practice's real appointment types?","What share of missed calls are new-patient calls, and when do they occur (lunch, after-hours, peak times)? This determines whether overflow-only coverage is enough.","Is there a Montgomery County or Silver Spring local rule, or a Maryland State Board of Dental Examiners position, on recorded or automated patient communications that counsel should confirm?","How will the single front-desk employee and the dentists react? Staff buy-in affects callbacks and booking corrections.","Do patients, including older patients and non-English speakers, accept an automated assistant? Is there a clear route to a human and to the on-call dentist for emergencies?","Is call recording needed at all, or could the practice use transcripts or summaries only to reduce consent and retention exposure?"]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/researcher@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/researcher@1"]},"id":"f2d50a54-42e0-4913-8bff-26c16c7bcfe2","timestamp":1794351852.2605934}

data: {"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{"evidence":{"decision":"Whether Bright Smile Family Dental should replace its front-desk phone line with an AI receptionist that answers calls, books appointments and records calls.","options":["Do nothing: keep the current RingCentral line and the one-person front desk, accepting the missed new-patient calls.","Run a two-week AI pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, then decide.","Fully replace the front-desk line with an AI receptionist that answers, books into Dentrix and records calls.","Use a hybrid or live dental answering service in which the software or agents take overflow and after-hours calls and hand harder calls to staff."],"facts":[{"claim":"Maryland is an all-party (two-party) consent state under its Wiretapping and Electronic Surveillance Act. Recording a private call without every party's consent can be charged as a felony, so an AI receptionist that records calls must play a disclosure and capture consent before recording starts.","source":"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:36Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"HIPAA requires a signed Business Associate Agreement and encrypted call data before an AI vendor handles patient information. Maryland's Confidentiality of Medical Records Act should also be treated as applying to call data, not just generic call-center rules.","source":"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:36Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"A vendor checklist for Maryland: a recording disclosure at the start of every call, stored proof of consent, a BAA signed before any patient data flows, encryption with configurable retention, and written answers to all of these.","source":"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:36Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Ask for the BAA on the exact plan being bought, not only a higher tier. Also ask for the sub-processor list (speech-to-text, language model, hosting), whether recordings or transcripts train any model, retention and deletion terms, who at the vendor can listen, and multifactor authentication on every login.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:37Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"An AI receptionist that books needs read-and-write access to the schedule. Hygiene recall, new-patient exam and emergency exam slots can each have different lengths, providers and chairs, so the vendor must be tested on the practice's real appointment types. A demo on the vendor's sample schedule proves little.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:37Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Commonly reported AI receptionist failure points are odd requests, accents or noise, and booking rules it was never taught. In hybrid setups the handoff between bot and staff is what usually fails first.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:37Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"A suggested two-week pilot on after-hours or overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, should measure answered-call rate, correct bookings, transfers to staff, emergency handoffs and patient complaints. The keep-or-drop criteria should be agreed with the vendor in advance.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:37Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"One vendor-published framework says some calls should always go to a human: post-op and clinical-concern calls, refund and billing disputes, and any patient who asks for a person. Another vendor source lists complex patient situations, complaints, multi-step scheduling and emotional calls as better suited to humans.","source":"https://hillflare.com/en/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-front-desk-2026","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:36Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"The AI receptionist software vendors' own case studies, such as AgentZap's 90-day side-by-side comparison, are marketing material. Their answered-call improvements are not independent benchmarks for a two-dentist practice.","source":"https://agentzap.ai/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-receptionist-90-day-comparison","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:37Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Typical AI receptionist pricing is per conversation, per minute or a flat monthly plan, and live answering services usually charge per minute or per call plus a base fee. Specific current plan prices were not retrieved from vendor pricing pages in this session, so no vendor price is confirmed against the $400/month budget.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"RingCentral, the practice's current phone provider, markets its own AI receptionist add-on. Whether it fits within budget and works with Dentrix should be checked on RingCentral's pricing page.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"FCC rules treat AI-generated voices as artificial voices under the TCPA for outbound calls. This matters only if the AI would make outbound reminder or recall calls rather than just answer inbound ones.","source":"","verified":false}],"open_questions":["What exact plan price, per-minute or per-call overage and contract term would each shortlisted vendor charge, and does the total stay under $400/month?","Will the vendor sign a BAA on the plan being purchased, and does it capture Maryland all-party recording consent at the start of every call, including after-hours and transfers?","Does the vendor have a supported Dentrix integration, and can it write new-patient and recall appointments with the practice's real appointment types?","What share of missed calls are new-patient calls, and when do they occur (lunch, after-hours, peak times)? This determines whether overflow-only coverage is enough.","Is there a Montgomery County or Silver Spring local rule, or a Maryland State Board of Dental Examiners position, on recorded or automated patient communications that counsel should confirm?","How will the single front-desk employee and the dentists react? Staff buy-in affects callbacks and booking corrections.","Do patients, including older patients and non-English speakers, accept an automated assistant? Is there a clear route to a human and to the on-call dentist for emergencies?","Is call recording needed at all, or could the practice use transcripts or summaries only to reduce consent and retention exposure?"]}},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"decision":"Whether Bright Smile Family Dental should replace its front-desk phone line with an AI receptionist that answers calls, books appointments and records calls.","options":["Do nothing: keep the current RingCentral line and the one-person front desk, accepting the missed new-patient calls.","Run a two-week AI pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, then decide.","Fully replace the front-desk line with an AI receptionist that answers, books into Dentrix and records calls.","Use a hybrid or live dental answering service in which the software or agents take overflow and after-hours calls and hand harder calls to staff."],"facts":[{"claim":"Maryland is an all-party (two-party) consent state under its Wiretapping and Electronic Surveillance Act. Recording a private call without every party's consent can be charged as a felony, so an AI receptionist that records calls must play a disclosure and capture consent before recording starts.","source":"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:36Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"HIPAA requires a signed Business Associate Agreement and encrypted call data before an AI vendor handles patient information. Maryland's Confidentiality of Medical Records Act should also be treated as applying to call data, not just generic call-center rules.","source":"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:36Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"A vendor checklist for Maryland: a recording disclosure at the start of every call, stored proof of consent, a BAA signed before any patient data flows, encryption with configurable retention, and written answers to all of these.","source":"https://www.dentalbase.ai/blogs/practice-management/ai-receptionist-for-dental-practices-maryland","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:36Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Ask for the BAA on the exact plan being bought, not only a higher tier. Also ask for the sub-processor list (speech-to-text, language model, hosting), whether recordings or transcripts train any model, retention and deletion terms, who at the vendor can listen, and multifactor authentication on every login.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:37Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"An AI receptionist that books needs read-and-write access to the schedule. Hygiene recall, new-patient exam and emergency exam slots can each have different lengths, providers and chairs, so the vendor must be tested on the practice's real appointment types. A demo on the vendor's sample schedule proves little.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:37Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Commonly reported AI receptionist failure points are odd requests, accents or noise, and booking rules it was never taught. In hybrid setups the handoff between bot and staff is what usually fails first.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:37Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"A suggested two-week pilot on after-hours or overflow calls only, with staff still answering the main line, should measure answered-call rate, correct bookings, transfers to staff, emergency handoffs and patient complaints. The keep-or-drop criteria should be agreed with the vendor in advance.","source":"https://www.legendnt.com/blog/dental-ai-receptionist-answering-service","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:37Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"One vendor-published framework says some calls should always go to a human: post-op and clinical-concern calls, refund and billing disputes, and any patient who asks for a person. Another vendor source lists complex patient situations, complaints, multi-step scheduling and emotional calls as better suited to humans.","source":"https://hillflare.com/en/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-front-desk-2026","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:36Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"The AI receptionist software vendors' own case studies, such as AgentZap's 90-day side-by-side comparison, are marketing material. Their answered-call improvements are not independent benchmarks for a two-dentist practice.","source":"https://agentzap.ai/blog/ai-receptionist-vs-human-receptionist-90-day-comparison","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:04:37Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Typical AI receptionist pricing is per conversation, per minute or a flat monthly plan, and live answering services usually charge per minute or per call plus a base fee. Specific current plan prices were not retrieved from vendor pricing pages in this session, so no vendor price is confirmed against the $400/month budget.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"RingCentral, the practice's current phone provider, markets its own AI receptionist add-on. Whether it fits within budget and works with Dentrix should be checked on RingCentral's pricing page.","source":"","verified":false},{"claim":"FCC rules treat AI-generated voices as artificial voices under the TCPA for outbound calls. This matters only if the AI would make outbound reminder or recall calls rather than just answer inbound ones.","source":"","verified":false}],"open_questions":["What exact plan price, per-minute or per-call overage and contract term would each shortlisted vendor charge, and does the total stay under $400/month?","Will the vendor sign a BAA on the plan being purchased, and does it capture Maryland all-party recording consent at the start of every call, including after-hours and transfers?","Does the vendor have a supported Dentrix integration, and can it write new-patient and recall appointments with the practice's real appointment types?","What share of missed calls are new-patient calls, and when do they occur (lunch, after-hours, peak times)? This determines whether overflow-only coverage is enough.","Is there a Montgomery County or Silver Spring local rule, or a Maryland State Board of Dental Examiners position, on recorded or automated patient communications that counsel should confirm?","How will the single front-desk employee and the dentists react? Staff buy-in affects callbacks and booking corrections.","Do patients, including older patients and non-English speakers, accept an automated assistant? Is there a clear route to a human and to the on-call dentist for emergencies?","Is call recording needed at all, or could the practice use transcripts or summaries only to reduce consent and retention exposure?"]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/check_sources@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/check_sources@1"]},"id":"649bc49b-df12-4542-81d2-9d3dd7268c1f","timestamp":1794351877.590854}

data: {"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":"ROUND 1 — give your independent opinion. You have not seen the other seats yet.","nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/convene@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/convene@1"]},"id":"55ab5900-a5fd-4d80-bf34-29910fbdb647","timestamp":1794351877.5937142}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"oppose\",\"headline\":\"Don't sign up this month; run a limited pilot first.\",\"summary\":\"Full replacement this month is premature. Vendor pricing, BAA terms, Maryland recording consent and Dentrix booking are all unconfirmed. A two-week overflow-only pilot with clear keep-or-drop rules tests the value before you commit the front desk and patient data.\",\"key_points\":[\"Maryland is all-party consent; recording without consent can be a felony, so disclosure and consent capture are mandatory (F1, F3).\",\"No vendor price has been confirmed against the $400 budget, and the BAA and Dentrix fit are unproven (F10, F4, F5).\",\"A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with success measures agreed in advance, shows real value at low risk (F7).\"],\"risks\":[\"Booking errors in Dentrix on real appointment types (new-patient exams, emergencies) could cause schedule chaos and lost revenue (F5).\",\"Handoff failures between bot and staff, the most common failure point, could drop urgent or clinical calls (F6, F8).\",\"Patient privacy or consent lapses could create HIPAA or Maryland exposure before the BAA and consent terms are verified (F1, F2).\"],\"confidence\":72,\"pushback\":\"Assumption to question: that missed new-patient calls are mostly due to no one answering. Find out how many are lost and when before paying for full coverage; overflow-only may be enough.}\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":488,"promptTokenCount":3318,"totalTokenCount":3806},"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"devils_advocate_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't sign up this month; run a limited pilot first.","summary":"Full replacement this month is premature. Vendor pricing, BAA terms, Maryland recording consent and Dentrix booking are all unconfirmed. A two-week overflow-only pilot with clear keep-or-drop rules tests the value before you commit the front desk and patient data.","key_points":["Maryland is all-party consent; recording without consent can be a felony, so disclosure and consent capture are mandatory (F1, F3).","No vendor price has been confirmed against the $400 budget, and the BAA and Dentrix fit are unproven (F10, F4, F5).","A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with success measures agreed in advance, shows real value at low risk (F7)."],"risks":["Booking errors in Dentrix on real appointment types (new-patient exams, emergencies) could cause schedule chaos and lost revenue (F5).","Handoff failures between bot and staff, the most common failure point, could drop urgent or clinical calls (F6, F8).","Patient privacy or consent lapses could create HIPAA or Maryland exposure before the BAA and consent terms are verified (F1, F2)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumption to question: that missed new-patient calls are mostly due to no one answering. Find out how many are lost and when before paying for full coverage; overflow-only may be enough.}"},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/devils_advocate_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/devils_advocate_r1@1"]},"branch":"devils_advocate_r1@1","id":"fc6c6bb4-be1b-4e9a-81a9-29d3aa25ef23","timestamp":1794351877.6057105}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Pilot only after-hours and overflow calls; no full replacement this month.\",\"summary\":\"Signing up for a full AI receptionist this month is premature. Legal exposure is real: Maryland requires every caller to consent to recording (F1), and patient data needs a signed BAA first (F2). Pricing against your $400 budget is unconfirmed (F10). A two-week after-hours and overflow pilot, with staff keeping the main line, tests the idea cheaply and gives you measured data before you commit (F7).\",\"key_points\":[\"Maryland all-party consent and HIPAA BAA rules must be met before any call is recorded or patient data flows (F1, F2).\",\"Vendor prices against the $400 budget are not yet confirmed; get written quotes first (F10, F11).\",\"A limited pilot with agreed keep-or-drop criteria shows whether it actually captures new-patient calls (F7).\"],\"risks\":[\"Recording calls without every caller's consent can be a felony under Maryland law (F1); the vendor must capture consent on every call, including transfers.\",\"Booking errors against Dentrix appointment types could double-book or misfill chairs (F5, F6).\",\"Patients with urgent clinical concerns or who ask for a person could be trapped in a bot (F8).\"],\"confidence\":72,\"pushback\":\"The assumption that an AI receptionist will capture the missed new-patient calls. The evidence on missed-call share and timing is missing (open questions), and vendor success figures are marketing (F9).\",\"checks\":[{\"area\":\"legal\",\"status\":\"block\",\"note\":\"Maryland all-party consent (F1) and a signed BAA on the exact purchased plan (F2, F4) must be in place before recording or patient data flows; have a healthcare attorney confirm, and check any Montgomery County or Maryland Board of Dental Examiners rules (unverified).\"},{\"area\":\"compliance\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Ask for encryption, sub-processor list, no-training terms, retention limits and MFA on all logins (F3, F4); confirm with the vendor and your IT provider (unverified).\"},{\"area\":\"financial\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"No vendor price is confirmed against $400/month (F10); RingCentral's add-on price is unverified (F11), so get written quotes including overage and contract term.\"},{\"area\":\"goals\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"note\":\"Overflow and after-hours coverage targets the stated goal of not missing new-patient calls (F7), but only if missed-call data confirms the gap.\"},{\"area\":\"operational\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Dentrix integration and real appointment-type testing are unverified (F5); the single front-desk employee needs a clear handoff plan (F6, F8).\"}]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":903,"promptTokenCount":3655,"totalTokenCount":4558},"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"realist_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot only after-hours and overflow calls; no full replacement this month.","summary":"Signing up for a full AI receptionist this month is premature. Legal exposure is real: Maryland requires every caller to consent to recording (F1), and patient data needs a signed BAA first (F2). Pricing against your $400 budget is unconfirmed (F10). A two-week after-hours and overflow pilot, with staff keeping the main line, tests the idea cheaply and gives you measured data before you commit (F7).","key_points":["Maryland all-party consent and HIPAA BAA rules must be met before any call is recorded or patient data flows (F1, F2).","Vendor prices against the $400 budget are not yet confirmed; get written quotes first (F10, F11).","A limited pilot with agreed keep-or-drop criteria shows whether it actually captures new-patient calls (F7)."],"risks":["Recording calls without every caller's consent can be a felony under Maryland law (F1); the vendor must capture consent on every call, including transfers.","Booking errors against Dentrix appointment types could double-book or misfill chairs (F5, F6).","Patients with urgent clinical concerns or who ask for a person could be trapped in a bot (F8)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"The assumption that an AI receptionist will capture the missed new-patient calls. The evidence on missed-call share and timing is missing (open questions), and vendor success figures are marketing (F9).","checks":[{"area":"legal","status":"block","note":"Maryland all-party consent (F1) and a signed BAA on the exact purchased plan (F2, F4) must be in place before recording or patient data flows; have a healthcare attorney confirm, and check any Montgomery County or Maryland Board of Dental Examiners rules (unverified)."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"Ask for encryption, sub-processor list, no-training terms, retention limits and MFA on all logins (F3, F4); confirm with the vendor and your IT provider (unverified)."},{"area":"financial","status":"flag","note":"No vendor price is confirmed against $400/month (F10); RingCentral's add-on price is unverified (F11), so get written quotes including overage and contract term."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"Overflow and after-hours coverage targets the stated goal of not missing new-patient calls (F7), but only if missed-call data confirms the gap."},{"area":"operational","status":"flag","note":"Dentrix integration and real appointment-type testing are unverified (F5); the single front-desk employee needs a clear handoff plan (F6, F8)."}]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/realist_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/realist_r1@1"]},"branch":"realist_r1@1","id":"a91edea1-3f04-4e6b-aab4-e258a5cf865c","timestamp":1794351877.6040475}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Pilot after-hours calls first; don't fully replace the front desk yet.\",\"summary\":\"Signing up this month is premature. Full replacement commits you to a contract, Dentrix integration and recording rules before you know the vendor fits your schedule. A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with agreed pass/fail measures, tests the claim cheaply. Confirm the BAA, consent and price first.\",\"key_points\":[\"Run the two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with keep-or-drop criteria agreed in advance (F7).\",\"Do not let any patient data flow until a signed BAA and Maryland all-party consent handling are confirmed in writing (F1, F2, F3).\",\"Price is unconfirmed against the $400 budget; get the total with overage and contract term before signing (F10, open questions).\"],\"risks\":[\"Booking errors on your real appointment types and the bot-to-staff handoff, which usually fails first (F5, F6).\",\"Some calls must reach a human, such as clinical concerns and billing disputes, so the bot cannot replace the front desk (F8).\",\"A signed annual contract could lock you in beyond the pilot if you never test the exit terms (unverified; confirm with the vendor and an attorney).\"],\"confidence\":70,\"pushback\":\"You assume most missed calls are new-patient calls that an AI can book. Check the share of missed calls and when they happen; if most fall at lunch or during staff hours, overflow-only coverage may not fix the problem.','\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":1017,"promptTokenCount":3265,"thoughtsTokenCount":508,"totalTokenCount":4282},"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"strategist_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours calls first; don't fully replace the front desk yet.","summary":"Signing up this month is premature. Full replacement commits you to a contract, Dentrix integration and recording rules before you know the vendor fits your schedule. A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with agreed pass/fail measures, tests the claim cheaply. Confirm the BAA, consent and price first.","key_points":["Run the two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with keep-or-drop criteria agreed in advance (F7).","Do not let any patient data flow until a signed BAA and Maryland all-party consent handling are confirmed in writing (F1, F2, F3).","Price is unconfirmed against the $400 budget; get the total with overage and contract term before signing (F10, open questions)."],"risks":["Booking errors on your real appointment types and the bot-to-staff handoff, which usually fails first (F5, F6).","Some calls must reach a human, such as clinical concerns and billing disputes, so the bot cannot replace the front desk (F8).","A signed annual contract could lock you in beyond the pilot if you never test the exit terms (unverified; confirm with the vendor and an attorney)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"You assume most missed calls are new-patient calls that an AI can book. Check the share of missed calls and when they happen; if most fall at lunch or during staff hours, overflow-only coverage may not fix the problem.','"},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/strategist_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/strategist_r1@1"]},"branch":"strategist_r1@1","id":"26aacc0c-8992-4131-88da-1023277ff715","timestamp":1794351877.6049762}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Conditional yes: pilot after-hours and overflow calls first, not full switch.\",\"summary\":\"Don't sign a full AI replacement this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls while staff keep the main line (F7). Require a signed BAA, Maryland consent capture, and a Dentrix booking test on your real appointment types before any full switch (F1, F2, F4, F5).\",\"key_points\":[\"Missed new-patient calls are the real problem, and a narrow pilot measures them cheaply (F7).\",\"The $400/month fit is unconfirmed; get written quotes including overage and contract term (F10).\",\"Vendor case-study gains are marketing, not proof for a two-dentist practice (F9).\"],\"risks\":[\"Wrong bookings in Dentrix on real appointment types, not vendor demo schedules (F5).\",\"Recording without all-party consent carries felony exposure (F1); have an attorney confirm the disclosure script.\",\"Failures at the bot-to-staff handoff, with one front-desk person absorbing the fallout (F6).\"],\"confidence\":70,\"pushback\":\"You assume a full switch frees front-desk time, but hard calls, complaints and booking fixes still need a human (F6, F8).\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":1639,"promptTokenCount":3261,"thoughtsTokenCount":1218,"totalTokenCount":4900},"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"industry_expert_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Conditional yes: pilot after-hours and overflow calls first, not full switch.","summary":"Don't sign a full AI replacement this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls while staff keep the main line (F7). Require a signed BAA, Maryland consent capture, and a Dentrix booking test on your real appointment types before any full switch (F1, F2, F4, F5).","key_points":["Missed new-patient calls are the real problem, and a narrow pilot measures them cheaply (F7).","The $400/month fit is unconfirmed; get written quotes including overage and contract term (F10).","Vendor case-study gains are marketing, not proof for a two-dentist practice (F9)."],"risks":["Wrong bookings in Dentrix on real appointment types, not vendor demo schedules (F5).","Recording without all-party consent carries felony exposure (F1); have an attorney confirm the disclosure script.","Failures at the bot-to-staff handoff, with one front-desk person absorbing the fallout (F6)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"You assume a full switch frees front-desk time, but hard calls, complaints and booking fixes still need a human (F6, F8)."},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/industry_expert_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/industry_expert_r1@1"]},"branch":"industry_expert_r1@1","id":"288cdca2-d04a-4a6b-8a0a-140f7138a03a","timestamp":1794351877.6008115}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls first; don't fully replace the line yet\",\"summary\":\"Missed new-patient calls are a real problem, but a full swap this month is premature. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still on the main line. Sign only if the vendor confirms a BAA, Maryland recording consent, and Dentrix booking on your real appointment types.\",\"key_points\":[\"Maryland is all-party consent, so any recording needs a disclosure and captured consent at every call (F1, F3).\",\"Booking needs a BAA on the exact plan bought and read-write Dentrix access, tested on your own appointment types (F2, F4, F5).\",\"Pilot on overflow only, with keep-or-drop criteria agreed in advance, limits risk while you measure results (F7).\"],\"risks\":[\"Handoffs between bot and front desk fail first, which could drop urgent or clinical calls (F6, F8).\",\"Vendor prices are unconfirmed, so the total could exceed the $400 budget once overages and contract terms apply (F10).\",\"Vendor case studies are marketing, not proof of results for a two-dentist practice (F9).\"],\"confidence\":68,\"pushback\":\"Assumes the missed calls are mostly new-patient calls that an AI can book. Nobody has measured how many are missed, or when; check that before paying for any tool.\",\"tools\":[{\"name\":\"RingCentral AI Receptionist\",\"use\":\"Adds an AI answering layer to the phone line you already use, to cover after-hours and overflow calls.\",\"monthly_cost\":\"unknown; check RingCentral pricing (F11)\",\"works_with\":\"Your existing RingCentral line; Dentrix booking integration unconfirmed.\",\"caveat\":\"Pricing and Dentrix fit are unverified (F11); ask for the BAA and consent flow in writing before use.\",\"url\":\"\",\"price_fact\":\"\",\"pilot_test\":\"Keep it if it books at least 5 new-patient appointments and transfers under 20% of calls to staff in 2 weeks on after-hours calls; cancel before the first invoice otherwise.\"},{\"name\":\"Weave\",\"use\":\"Dental phone and messaging platform that can handle missed-call texting and basic scheduling for dental offices.\",\"monthly_cost\":\"$0-400/mo estimate (unverified)\",\"works_with\":\"Designed for dental practices; check Dentrix integration before buying.\",\"caveat\":\"Its AI booking depth and exact Dentrix support are unverified; confirm BAA and consent handling first.\",\"url\":\"\",\"price_fact\":\"\",\"pilot_test\":\"Keep it if missed-call recovery converts at least 5 extra new-patient bookings in 4 weeks; decide before the second invoice.\"}]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":1324,"promptTokenCount":4292,"thoughtsTokenCount":413,"totalTokenCount":5616},"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"ai_tools_expert_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls first; don't fully replace the line yet","summary":"Missed new-patient calls are a real problem, but a full swap this month is premature. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still on the main line. Sign only if the vendor confirms a BAA, Maryland recording consent, and Dentrix booking on your real appointment types.","key_points":["Maryland is all-party consent, so any recording needs a disclosure and captured consent at every call (F1, F3).","Booking needs a BAA on the exact plan bought and read-write Dentrix access, tested on your own appointment types (F2, F4, F5).","Pilot on overflow only, with keep-or-drop criteria agreed in advance, limits risk while you measure results (F7)."],"risks":["Handoffs between bot and front desk fail first, which could drop urgent or clinical calls (F6, F8).","Vendor prices are unconfirmed, so the total could exceed the $400 budget once overages and contract terms apply (F10).","Vendor case studies are marketing, not proof of results for a two-dentist practice (F9)."],"confidence":68,"pushback":"Assumes the missed calls are mostly new-patient calls that an AI can book. Nobody has measured how many are missed, or when; check that before paying for any tool.","tools":[{"name":"RingCentral AI Receptionist","use":"Adds an AI answering layer to the phone line you already use, to cover after-hours and overflow calls.","monthly_cost":"unknown; check RingCentral pricing (F11)","works_with":"Your existing RingCentral line; Dentrix booking integration unconfirmed.","caveat":"Pricing and Dentrix fit are unverified (F11); ask for the BAA and consent flow in writing before use.","url":"","price_fact":"","pilot_test":"Keep it if it books at least 5 new-patient appointments and transfers under 20% of calls to staff in 2 weeks on after-hours calls; cancel before the first invoice otherwise."},{"name":"Weave","use":"Dental phone and messaging platform that can handle missed-call texting and basic scheduling for dental offices.","monthly_cost":"$0-400/mo estimate (unverified)","works_with":"Designed for dental practices; check Dentrix integration before buying.","caveat":"Its AI booking depth and exact Dentrix support are unverified; confirm BAA and consent handling first.","url":"","price_fact":"","pilot_test":"Keep it if missed-call recovery converts at least 5 extra new-patient bookings in 4 weeks; decide before the second invoice."}]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/ai_tools_expert_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/ai_tools_expert_r1@1"]},"branch":"ai_tools_expert_r1@1","id":"46a48e81-b462-4904-8fb2-a206c3e27a63","timestamp":1794351877.6018956}

data: {"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"industry_expert_r1":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Conditional yes: pilot after-hours and overflow calls first, not full switch.","summary":"Don't sign a full AI replacement this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls while staff keep the main line (F7). Require a signed BAA, Maryland consent capture, and a Dentrix booking test on your real appointment types before any full switch (F1, F2, F4, F5).","key_points":["Missed new-patient calls are the real problem, and a narrow pilot measures them cheaply (F7).","The $400/month fit is unconfirmed; get written quotes including overage and contract term (F10).","Vendor case-study gains are marketing, not proof for a two-dentist practice (F9)."],"risks":["Wrong bookings in Dentrix on real appointment types, not vendor demo schedules (F5).","Recording without all-party consent carries felony exposure (F1); have an attorney confirm the disclosure script.","Failures at the bot-to-staff handoff, with one front-desk person absorbing the fallout (F6)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"You assume a full switch frees front-desk time, but hard calls, complaints and booking fixes still need a human (F6, F8)."},"ai_tools_expert_r1":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls first; don't fully replace the line yet","summary":"Missed new-patient calls are a real problem, but a full swap this month is premature. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still on the main line. Sign only if the vendor confirms a BAA, Maryland recording consent, and Dentrix booking on your real appointment types.","key_points":["Maryland is all-party consent, so any recording needs a disclosure and captured consent at every call (F1, F3).","Booking needs a BAA on the exact plan bought and read-write Dentrix access, tested on your own appointment types (F2, F4, F5).","Pilot on overflow only, with keep-or-drop criteria agreed in advance, limits risk while you measure results (F7)."],"risks":["Handoffs between bot and front desk fail first, which could drop urgent or clinical calls (F6, F8).","Vendor prices are unconfirmed, so the total could exceed the $400 budget once overages and contract terms apply (F10).","Vendor case studies are marketing, not proof of results for a two-dentist practice (F9)."],"confidence":68,"pushback":"Assumes the missed calls are mostly new-patient calls that an AI can book. Nobody has measured how many are missed, or when; check that before paying for any tool.","tools":[{"name":"RingCentral AI Receptionist","use":"Adds an AI answering layer to the phone line you already use, to cover after-hours and overflow calls.","monthly_cost":"unknown; check RingCentral pricing (F11)","works_with":"Your existing RingCentral line; Dentrix booking integration unconfirmed.","caveat":"Pricing and Dentrix fit are unverified (F11); ask for the BAA and consent flow in writing before use.","url":"","price_fact":"","pilot_test":"Keep it if it books at least 5 new-patient appointments and transfers under 20% of calls to staff in 2 weeks on after-hours calls; cancel before the first invoice otherwise."},{"name":"Weave","use":"Dental phone and messaging platform that can handle missed-call texting and basic scheduling for dental offices.","monthly_cost":"$0-400/mo estimate (unverified)","works_with":"Designed for dental practices; check Dentrix integration before buying.","caveat":"Its AI booking depth and exact Dentrix support are unverified; confirm BAA and consent handling first.","url":"","price_fact":"","pilot_test":"Keep it if missed-call recovery converts at least 5 extra new-patient bookings in 4 weeks; decide before the second invoice."}]},"realist_r1":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot only after-hours and overflow calls; no full replacement this month.","summary":"Signing up for a full AI receptionist this month is premature. Legal exposure is real: Maryland requires every caller to consent to recording (F1), and patient data needs a signed BAA first (F2). Pricing against your $400 budget is unconfirmed (F10). A two-week after-hours and overflow pilot, with staff keeping the main line, tests the idea cheaply and gives you measured data before you commit (F7).","key_points":["Maryland all-party consent and HIPAA BAA rules must be met before any call is recorded or patient data flows (F1, F2).","Vendor prices against the $400 budget are not yet confirmed; get written quotes first (F10, F11).","A limited pilot with agreed keep-or-drop criteria shows whether it actually captures new-patient calls (F7)."],"risks":["Recording calls without every caller's consent can be a felony under Maryland law (F1); the vendor must capture consent on every call, including transfers.","Booking errors against Dentrix appointment types could double-book or misfill chairs (F5, F6).","Patients with urgent clinical concerns or who ask for a person could be trapped in a bot (F8)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"The assumption that an AI receptionist will capture the missed new-patient calls. The evidence on missed-call share and timing is missing (open questions), and vendor success figures are marketing (F9).","checks":[{"area":"legal","status":"block","note":"Maryland all-party consent (F1) and a signed BAA on the exact purchased plan (F2, F4) must be in place before recording or patient data flows; have a healthcare attorney confirm, and check any Montgomery County or Maryland Board of Dental Examiners rules (unverified)."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"Ask for encryption, sub-processor list, no-training terms, retention limits and MFA on all logins (F3, F4); confirm with the vendor and your IT provider (unverified)."},{"area":"financial","status":"flag","note":"No vendor price is confirmed against $400/month (F10); RingCentral's add-on price is unverified (F11), so get written quotes including overage and contract term."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"Overflow and after-hours coverage targets the stated goal of not missing new-patient calls (F7), but only if missed-call data confirms the gap."},{"area":"operational","status":"flag","note":"Dentrix integration and real appointment-type testing are unverified (F5); the single front-desk employee needs a clear handoff plan (F6, F8)."}]},"strategist_r1":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours calls first; don't fully replace the front desk yet.","summary":"Signing up this month is premature. Full replacement commits you to a contract, Dentrix integration and recording rules before you know the vendor fits your schedule. A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with agreed pass/fail measures, tests the claim cheaply. Confirm the BAA, consent and price first.","key_points":["Run the two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with keep-or-drop criteria agreed in advance (F7).","Do not let any patient data flow until a signed BAA and Maryland all-party consent handling are confirmed in writing (F1, F2, F3).","Price is unconfirmed against the $400 budget; get the total with overage and contract term before signing (F10, open questions)."],"risks":["Booking errors on your real appointment types and the bot-to-staff handoff, which usually fails first (F5, F6).","Some calls must reach a human, such as clinical concerns and billing disputes, so the bot cannot replace the front desk (F8).","A signed annual contract could lock you in beyond the pilot if you never test the exit terms (unverified; confirm with the vendor and an attorney)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"You assume most missed calls are new-patient calls that an AI can book. Check the share of missed calls and when they happen; if most fall at lunch or during staff hours, overflow-only coverage may not fix the problem.','"},"devils_advocate_r1":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't sign up this month; run a limited pilot first.","summary":"Full replacement this month is premature. Vendor pricing, BAA terms, Maryland recording consent and Dentrix booking are all unconfirmed. A two-week overflow-only pilot with clear keep-or-drop rules tests the value before you commit the front desk and patient data.","key_points":["Maryland is all-party consent; recording without consent can be a felony, so disclosure and consent capture are mandatory (F1, F3).","No vendor price has been confirmed against the $400 budget, and the BAA and Dentrix fit are unproven (F10, F4, F5).","A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with success measures agreed in advance, shows real value at low risk (F7)."],"risks":["Booking errors in Dentrix on real appointment types (new-patient exams, emergencies) could cause schedule chaos and lost revenue (F5).","Handoff failures between bot and staff, the most common failure point, could drop urgent or clinical calls (F6, F8).","Patient privacy or consent lapses could create HIPAA or Maryland exposure before the BAA and consent terms are verified (F1, F2)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumption to question: that missed new-patient calls are mostly due to no one answering. Find out how many are lost and when before paying for full coverage; overflow-only may be enough.}"}},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/round1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/round1@1"]},"id":"bdb30e71-94d4-4169-8a61-a53fd3b84fe5","timestamp":1794351886.0522263}

data: {"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{"round1":{"industry_expert":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Conditional yes: pilot after-hours and overflow calls first, not full switch.","summary":"Don't sign a full AI replacement this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls while staff keep the main line (F7). Require a signed BAA, Maryland consent capture, and a Dentrix booking test on your real appointment types before any full switch (F1, F2, F4, F5).","key_points":["Missed new-patient calls are the real problem, and a narrow pilot measures them cheaply (F7).","The $400/month fit is unconfirmed; get written quotes including overage and contract term (F10).","Vendor case-study gains are marketing, not proof for a two-dentist practice (F9)."],"risks":["Wrong bookings in Dentrix on real appointment types, not vendor demo schedules (F5).","Recording without all-party consent carries felony exposure (F1); have an attorney confirm the disclosure script.","Failures at the bot-to-staff handoff, with one front-desk person absorbing the fallout (F6)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"You assume a full switch frees front-desk time, but hard calls, complaints and booking fixes still need a human (F6, F8)."},"ai_tools_expert":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls first; don't fully replace the line yet","summary":"Missed new-patient calls are a real problem, but a full swap this month is premature. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still on the main line. Sign only if the vendor confirms a BAA, Maryland recording consent, and Dentrix booking on your real appointment types.","key_points":["Maryland is all-party consent, so any recording needs a disclosure and captured consent at every call (F1, F3).","Booking needs a BAA on the exact plan bought and read-write Dentrix access, tested on your own appointment types (F2, F4, F5).","Pilot on overflow only, with keep-or-drop criteria agreed in advance, limits risk while you measure results (F7)."],"risks":["Handoffs between bot and front desk fail first, which could drop urgent or clinical calls (F6, F8).","Vendor prices are unconfirmed, so the total could exceed the $400 budget once overages and contract terms apply (F10).","Vendor case studies are marketing, not proof of results for a two-dentist practice (F9)."],"confidence":68,"pushback":"Assumes the missed calls are mostly new-patient calls that an AI can book. Nobody has measured how many are missed, or when; check that before paying for any tool.","tools":[{"name":"RingCentral AI Receptionist","use":"Adds an AI answering layer to the phone line you already use, to cover after-hours and overflow calls.","monthly_cost":"unknown; check RingCentral pricing (F11)","works_with":"Your existing RingCentral line; Dentrix booking integration unconfirmed.","caveat":"Pricing and Dentrix fit are unverified (F11); ask for the BAA and consent flow in writing before use.","url":"","price_fact":"","pilot_test":"Keep it if it books at least 5 new-patient appointments and transfers under 20% of calls to staff in 2 weeks on after-hours calls; cancel before the first invoice otherwise."},{"name":"Weave","use":"Dental phone and messaging platform that can handle missed-call texting and basic scheduling for dental offices.","monthly_cost":"$0-400/mo estimate (unverified)","works_with":"Designed for dental practices; check Dentrix integration before buying.","caveat":"Its AI booking depth and exact Dentrix support are unverified; confirm BAA and consent handling first.","url":"","price_fact":"","pilot_test":"Keep it if missed-call recovery converts at least 5 extra new-patient bookings in 4 weeks; decide before the second invoice."}]},"realist":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot only after-hours and overflow calls; no full replacement this month.","summary":"Signing up for a full AI receptionist this month is premature. Legal exposure is real: Maryland requires every caller to consent to recording (F1), and patient data needs a signed BAA first (F2). Pricing against your $400 budget is unconfirmed (F10). A two-week after-hours and overflow pilot, with staff keeping the main line, tests the idea cheaply and gives you measured data before you commit (F7).","key_points":["Maryland all-party consent and HIPAA BAA rules must be met before any call is recorded or patient data flows (F1, F2).","Vendor prices against the $400 budget are not yet confirmed; get written quotes first (F10, F11).","A limited pilot with agreed keep-or-drop criteria shows whether it actually captures new-patient calls (F7)."],"risks":["Recording calls without every caller's consent can be a felony under Maryland law (F1); the vendor must capture consent on every call, including transfers.","Booking errors against Dentrix appointment types could double-book or misfill chairs (F5, F6).","Patients with urgent clinical concerns or who ask for a person could be trapped in a bot (F8)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"The assumption that an AI receptionist will capture the missed new-patient calls. The evidence on missed-call share and timing is missing (open questions), and vendor success figures are marketing (F9).","checks":[{"area":"legal","status":"block","note":"Maryland all-party consent (F1) and a signed BAA on the exact purchased plan (F2, F4) must be in place before recording or patient data flows; have a healthcare attorney confirm, and check any Montgomery County or Maryland Board of Dental Examiners rules (unverified)."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"Ask for encryption, sub-processor list, no-training terms, retention limits and MFA on all logins (F3, F4); confirm with the vendor and your IT provider (unverified)."},{"area":"financial","status":"flag","note":"No vendor price is confirmed against $400/month (F10); RingCentral's add-on price is unverified (F11), so get written quotes including overage and contract term."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"Overflow and after-hours coverage targets the stated goal of not missing new-patient calls (F7), but only if missed-call data confirms the gap."},{"area":"operational","status":"flag","note":"Dentrix integration and real appointment-type testing are unverified (F5); the single front-desk employee needs a clear handoff plan (F6, F8)."}]},"strategist":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours calls first; don't fully replace the front desk yet.","summary":"Signing up this month is premature. Full replacement commits you to a contract, Dentrix integration and recording rules before you know the vendor fits your schedule. A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with agreed pass/fail measures, tests the claim cheaply. Confirm the BAA, consent and price first.","key_points":["Run the two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with keep-or-drop criteria agreed in advance (F7).","Do not let any patient data flow until a signed BAA and Maryland all-party consent handling are confirmed in writing (F1, F2, F3).","Price is unconfirmed against the $400 budget; get the total with overage and contract term before signing (F10, open questions)."],"risks":["Booking errors on your real appointment types and the bot-to-staff handoff, which usually fails first (F5, F6).","Some calls must reach a human, such as clinical concerns and billing disputes, so the bot cannot replace the front desk (F8).","A signed annual contract could lock you in beyond the pilot if you never test the exit terms (unverified; confirm with the vendor and an attorney)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"You assume most missed calls are new-patient calls that an AI can book. Check the share of missed calls and when they happen; if most fall at lunch or during staff hours, overflow-only coverage may not fix the problem.','"},"devils_advocate":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't sign up this month; run a limited pilot first.","summary":"Full replacement this month is premature. Vendor pricing, BAA terms, Maryland recording consent and Dentrix booking are all unconfirmed. A two-week overflow-only pilot with clear keep-or-drop rules tests the value before you commit the front desk and patient data.","key_points":["Maryland is all-party consent; recording without consent can be a felony, so disclosure and consent capture are mandatory (F1, F3).","No vendor price has been confirmed against the $400 budget, and the BAA and Dentrix fit are unproven (F10, F4, F5).","A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with success measures agreed in advance, shows real value at low risk (F7)."],"risks":["Booking errors in Dentrix on real appointment types (new-patient exams, emergencies) could cause schedule chaos and lost revenue (F5).","Handoff failures between bot and staff, the most common failure point, could drop urgent or clinical calls (F6, F8).","Patient privacy or consent lapses could create HIPAA or Maryland exposure before the BAA and consent terms are verified (F1, F2)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumption to question: that missed new-patient calls are mostly due to no one answering. Find out how many are lost and when before paying for full coverage; overflow-only may be enough.}"}}},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":"ROUND 2 — rebuttal. Below is every seat's Round 1 position. Respond to other seats by name. Change your stance only if a specific argument persuades you, and name it in changed_because. Do not drift toward agreement for its own sake. Return your full updated opinion.\n\n## Round 1\n\n### Industry Expert (industry_expert) — conditional, confidence 70\nHeadline: Conditional yes: pilot after-hours and overflow calls first, not full switch.\nSummary: Don't sign a full AI replacement this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls while staff keep the main line (F7). Require a signed BAA, Maryland consent capture, and a Dentrix booking test on your real appointment types before any full switch (F1, F2, F4, F5).\nPoints: Missed new-patient calls are the real problem, and a narrow pilot measures them cheaply (F7). | The $400/month fit is unconfirmed; get written quotes including overage and contract term (F10). | Vendor case-study gains are marketing, not proof for a two-dentist practice (F9).\nRisks: Wrong bookings in Dentrix on real appointment types, not vendor demo schedules (F5). | Recording without all-party consent carries felony exposure (F1); have an attorney confirm the disclosure script. | Failures at the bot-to-staff handoff, with one front-desk person absorbing the fallout (F6).\nPushback: You assume a full switch frees front-desk time, but hard calls, complaints and booking fixes still need a human (F6, F8).\n\n### AI-Tools Expert (ai_tools_expert) — conditional, confidence 68\nHeadline: Pilot after-hours and overflow calls first; don't fully replace the line yet\nSummary: Missed new-patient calls are a real problem, but a full swap this month is premature. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff still on the main line. Sign only if the vendor confirms a BAA, Maryland recording consent, and Dentrix booking on your real appointment types.\nPoints: Maryland is all-party consent, so any recording needs a disclosure and captured consent at every call (F1, F3). | Booking needs a BAA on the exact plan bought and read-write Dentrix access, tested on your own appointment types (F2, F4, F5). | Pilot on overflow only, with keep-or-drop criteria agreed in advance, limits risk while you measure results (F7).\nRisks: Handoffs between bot and front desk fail first, which could drop urgent or clinical calls (F6, F8). | Vendor prices are unconfirmed, so the total could exceed the $400 budget once overages and contract terms apply (F10). | Vendor case studies are marketing, not proof of results for a two-dentist practice (F9).\nPushback: Assumes the missed calls are mostly new-patient calls that an AI can book. Nobody has measured how many are missed, or when; check that before paying for any tool.\nTool: RingCentral AI Receptionist (unknown; check RingCentral pricing (F11)) — Adds an AI answering layer to the phone line you already use, to cover after-hours and overflow calls. Caveat: Pricing and Dentrix fit are unverified (F11); ask for the BAA and consent flow in writing before use. Pilot test: Keep it if it books at least 5 new-patient appointments and transfers under 20% of calls to staff in 2 weeks on after-hours calls; cancel before the first invoice otherwise.\nTool: Weave ($0-400/mo estimate (unverified)) — Dental phone and messaging platform that can handle missed-call texting and basic scheduling for dental offices. Caveat: Its AI booking depth and exact Dentrix support are unverified; confirm BAA and consent handling first. Pilot test: Keep it if missed-call recovery converts at least 5 extra new-patient bookings in 4 weeks; decide before the second invoice.\n\n### Realist (realist) — conditional, confidence 72\nHeadline: Pilot only after-hours and overflow calls; no full replacement this month.\nSummary: Signing up for a full AI receptionist this month is premature. Legal exposure is real: Maryland requires every caller to consent to recording (F1), and patient data needs a signed BAA first (F2). Pricing against your $400 budget is unconfirmed (F10). A two-week after-hours and overflow pilot, with staff keeping the main line, tests the idea cheaply and gives you measured data before you commit (F7).\nPoints: Maryland all-party consent and HIPAA BAA rules must be met before any call is recorded or patient data flows (F1, F2). | Vendor prices against the $400 budget are not yet confirmed; get written quotes first (F10, F11). | A limited pilot with agreed keep-or-drop criteria shows whether it actually captures new-patient calls (F7).\nRisks: Recording calls without every caller's consent can be a felony under Maryland law (F1); the vendor must capture consent on every call, including transfers. | Booking errors against Dentrix appointment types could double-book or misfill chairs (F5, F6). | Patients with urgent clinical concerns or who ask for a person could be trapped in a bot (F8).\nPushback: The assumption that an AI receptionist will capture the missed new-patient calls. The evidence on missed-call share and timing is missing (open questions), and vendor success figures are marketing (F9).\nCheck [legal: block] Maryland all-party consent (F1) and a signed BAA on the exact purchased plan (F2, F4) must be in place before recording or patient data flows; have a healthcare attorney confirm, and check any Montgomery County or Maryland Board of Dental Examiners rules (unverified).\nCheck [compliance: flag] Ask for encryption, sub-processor list, no-training terms, retention limits and MFA on all logins (F3, F4); confirm with the vendor and your IT provider (unverified).\nCheck [financial: flag] No vendor price is confirmed against $400/month (F10); RingCentral's add-on price is unverified (F11), so get written quotes including overage and contract term.\nCheck [goals: pass] Overflow and after-hours coverage targets the stated goal of not missing new-patient calls (F7), but only if missed-call data confirms the gap.\nCheck [operational: flag] Dentrix integration and real appointment-type testing are unverified (F5); the single front-desk employee needs a clear handoff plan (F6, F8).\n\n### Strategist (strategist) — conditional, confidence 70\nHeadline: Pilot after-hours calls first; don't fully replace the front desk yet.\nSummary: Signing up this month is premature. Full replacement commits you to a contract, Dentrix integration and recording rules before you know the vendor fits your schedule. A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with agreed pass/fail measures, tests the claim cheaply. Confirm the BAA, consent and price first.\nPoints: Run the two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with keep-or-drop criteria agreed in advance (F7). | Do not let any patient data flow until a signed BAA and Maryland all-party consent handling are confirmed in writing (F1, F2, F3). | Price is unconfirmed against the $400 budget; get the total with overage and contract term before signing (F10, open questions).\nRisks: Booking errors on your real appointment types and the bot-to-staff handoff, which usually fails first (F5, F6). | Some calls must reach a human, such as clinical concerns and billing disputes, so the bot cannot replace the front desk (F8). | A signed annual contract could lock you in beyond the pilot if you never test the exit terms (unverified; confirm with the vendor and an attorney).\nPushback: You assume most missed calls are new-patient calls that an AI can book. Check the share of missed calls and when they happen; if most fall at lunch or during staff hours, overflow-only coverage may not fix the problem.','\n\n### Devil's Advocate (devils_advocate) — oppose, confidence 72\nHeadline: Don't sign up this month; run a limited pilot first.\nSummary: Full replacement this month is premature. Vendor pricing, BAA terms, Maryland recording consent and Dentrix booking are all unconfirmed. A two-week overflow-only pilot with clear keep-or-drop rules tests the value before you commit the front desk and patient data.\nPoints: Maryland is all-party consent; recording without consent can be a felony, so disclosure and consent capture are mandatory (F1, F3). | No vendor price has been confirmed against the $400 budget, and the BAA and Dentrix fit are unproven (F10, F4, F5). | A two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, with success measures agreed in advance, shows real value at low risk (F7).\nRisks: Booking errors in Dentrix on real appointment types (new-patient exams, emergencies) could cause schedule chaos and lost revenue (F5). | Handoff failures between bot and staff, the most common failure point, could drop urgent or clinical calls (F6, F8). | Patient privacy or consent lapses could create HIPAA or Maryland exposure before the BAA and consent terms are verified (F1, F2).\nPushback: Assumption to question: that missed new-patient calls are mostly due to no one answering. Find out how many are lost and when before paying for full coverage; overflow-only may be enough.}","nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/open_rebuttal@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/open_rebuttal@1"]},"id":"c378e42a-f4d3-422c-8a88-c0cccb64dd81","timestamp":1794351886.0540934}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"changed_position\":false,\"changed_because\":\"\",\"rebuttals\":[{\"target\":\"ai_tools_expert\",\"their_claim\":\"Weave could add missed-call texting and basic scheduling as a lower-cost option.\",\"response\":\"Weave's $0-400 price is an unverified estimate (unverified). Its BAA, recording consent and Dentrix support must be confirmed in writing before it is treated as a cheap alternative. Any pilot must still capture Maryland consent (F1).\"},{\"target\":\"strategist\",\"their_claim\":\"A signed annual contract could lock the practice in beyond the pilot.\",\"response\":\"Agreed on the lock-in risk. Require month-to-month terms or a written exit clause before any pilot starts, and have an attorney review the contract (unverified).\"},{\"target\":\"devils_advocate\",\"their_claim\":\"Missed new-patient calls may be mostly due to no one answering, so overflow-only may be enough.\",\"response\":\"The data is missing. Without the share and timing of missed calls (open questions), overflow-only coverage may miss lunch-hour losses. Measure first, as F7 suggests.\"}],\"concessions\":[\"Overflow-only coverage may not fix lunch-hour or staff-hour misses; the pilot must measure timing, not just volume.\",\"Weave is a plausible alternative to test, not only a full AI receptionist.\"],\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls only, after BAA and consent are confirmed.\",\"summary\":\"Do not sign a full AI receptionist this month. Maryland requires every caller's consent before recording (F1), and a signed BAA must cover the exact plan bought (F2, F4). Neither the price against your $400 budget nor the Dentrix fit is confirmed (F10, F5). A two-week after-hours and overflow pilot, with agreed keep-or-drop rules, tests the idea cheaply (F7).\",\"key_points\":[\"Legal gate: Maryland all-party consent and a signed BAA on the purchased plan must be in writing first (F1, F2, F4).\",\"Money gate: get total monthly cost with overage and contract term; no vendor price is confirmed against $400 (F10, F11).\",\"Operations gate: test Dentrix booking on your real appointment types and map the human handoff (F5, F6, F8).\"],\"risks\":[\"Recording without every caller's consent can be a felony in Maryland (F1).\",\"Wrong bookings in Dentrix could double-book or misfill chairs (F5).\",\"Urgent clinical or 'ask for a person' calls trapped in a bot (F8).\"],\"confidence\":72,\"pushback\":\"Assume missed new-patient calls are mostly unanswered calls that an AI can book. Measure how many are missed and when before paying for any coverage (open questions, F9).\",\"checks\":[{\"area\":\"legal\",\"status\":\"block\",\"note\":\"Maryland all-party consent (F1) and a signed BAA on the exact plan (F2, F4) must exist before recording or patient data flows; have a healthcare attorney confirm, and check any Montgomery County or Maryland Board of Dental Examiners rules (unverified).\"},{\"area\":\"compliance\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Require encryption, sub-processor list, no-training terms, retention limits and MFA on all logins (F3, F4); confirm with the vendor and your IT provider (unverified).\"},{\"area\":\"financial\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"No vendor price is confirmed against $400/month (F10); RingCentral's add-on price is unverified (F11), so get written quotes including overage and contract term.\"},{\"area\":\"goals\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"note\":\"Overflow and after-hours coverage targets missed new-patient calls (F7), but only if missed-call data confirms the gap.\"},{\"area\":\"operational\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Dentrix integration and real appointment-type testing are unverified (F5); the single front-desk employee needs a clear handoff plan (F6, F8).\"}]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":1306,"promptTokenCount":7342,"totalTokenCount":8648},"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"realist_r2","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"ai_tools_expert","their_claim":"Weave could add missed-call texting and basic scheduling as a lower-cost option.","response":"Weave's $0-400 price is an unverified estimate (unverified). Its BAA, recording consent and Dentrix support must be confirmed in writing before it is treated as a cheap alternative. Any pilot must still capture Maryland consent (F1)."},{"target":"strategist","their_claim":"A signed annual contract could lock the practice in beyond the pilot.","response":"Agreed on the lock-in risk. Require month-to-month terms or a written exit clause before any pilot starts, and have an attorney review the contract (unverified)."},{"target":"devils_advocate","their_claim":"Missed new-patient calls may be mostly due to no one answering, so overflow-only may be enough.","response":"The data is missing. Without the share and timing of missed calls (open questions), overflow-only coverage may miss lunch-hour losses. Measure first, as F7 suggests."}],"concessions":["Overflow-only coverage may not fix lunch-hour or staff-hour misses; the pilot must measure timing, not just volume.","Weave is a plausible alternative to test, not only a full AI receptionist."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls only, after BAA and consent are confirmed.","summary":"Do not sign a full AI receptionist this month. Maryland requires every caller's consent before recording (F1), and a signed BAA must cover the exact plan bought (F2, F4). Neither the price against your $400 budget nor the Dentrix fit is confirmed (F10, F5). A two-week after-hours and overflow pilot, with agreed keep-or-drop rules, tests the idea cheaply (F7).","key_points":["Legal gate: Maryland all-party consent and a signed BAA on the purchased plan must be in writing first (F1, F2, F4).","Money gate: get total monthly cost with overage and contract term; no vendor price is confirmed against $400 (F10, F11).","Operations gate: test Dentrix booking on your real appointment types and map the human handoff (F5, F6, F8)."],"risks":["Recording without every caller's consent can be a felony in Maryland (F1).","Wrong bookings in Dentrix could double-book or misfill chairs (F5).","Urgent clinical or 'ask for a person' calls trapped in a bot (F8)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assume missed new-patient calls are mostly unanswered calls that an AI can book. Measure how many are missed and when before paying for any coverage (open questions, F9).","checks":[{"area":"legal","status":"block","note":"Maryland all-party consent (F1) and a signed BAA on the exact plan (F2, F4) must exist before recording or patient data flows; have a healthcare attorney confirm, and check any Montgomery County or Maryland Board of Dental Examiners rules (unverified)."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"Require encryption, sub-processor list, no-training terms, retention limits and MFA on all logins (F3, F4); confirm with the vendor and your IT provider (unverified)."},{"area":"financial","status":"flag","note":"No vendor price is confirmed against $400/month (F10); RingCentral's add-on price is unverified (F11), so get written quotes including overage and contract term."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"Overflow and after-hours coverage targets missed new-patient calls (F7), but only if missed-call data confirms the gap."},{"area":"operational","status":"flag","note":"Dentrix integration and real appointment-type testing are unverified (F5); the single front-desk employee needs a clear handoff plan (F6, F8)."}]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/realist_r2@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/realist_r2@1"]},"branch":"realist_r2@1","id":"21bb8e4e-ca13-47ef-9dd1-bb31c354c4d9","timestamp":1794351886.064427}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"changed_position\":false,\"changed_because\":\"\",\"rebuttals\":[{\"target\":\"devils_advocate\",\"their_claim\":\"Missed new-patient calls are mostly due to no one answering, so find out how many are lost and when before paying for full coverage.\",\"response\":\"Agreed on measuring first. A dental office's missed calls often cluster at lunch and during peak hours, not only after hours, so the pilot should log time of day too (F7). Overflow-only coverage may not reach those gaps.\"},{\"target\":\"strategist\",\"their_claim\":\"A signed annual contract could lock the practice in beyond the pilot if exit terms are never tested.\",\"response\":\"Right, and this matters more than the price. Before any pilot, get a month-to-month or pilot-length term in writing. Avoid annual prepayment until the keep-or-drop results are in (F7, F10).\"}],\"concessions\":[\"Missed-call share and timing are unmeasured, so overflow-only coverage may not fix the real gap.\",\"Annual contract lock-in is a real risk; exit terms should be negotiated before signing.\"],\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Conditional yes: pilot after-hours and overflow calls before any full switch.\",\"summary\":\"Don't sign a full AI replacement this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls while staff keep the main line. Log missed calls by time of day so you know where the gap is. Require a signed BAA, Maryland consent capture, a Dentrix booking test and written pricing with a short exit term before any full switch.\",\"key_points\":[\"Missed new-patient calls are the real problem, and a narrow pilot measures them cheaply (F7).\",\"Pilot pass/fail measures should include transfers to staff and booking errors on real appointment types (F5, F7).\",\"Budget fit and contract exit terms are unconfirmed; get written quotes first (F10).\"],\"risks\":[\"Wrong bookings in Dentrix on real appointment types, such as new-patient exams and emergencies (F5).\",\"Recording without all-party consent carries felony exposure in Maryland; have an attorney confirm the disclosure script (F1).\",\"Bot-to-staff handoff failures could land on the one front-desk person (F6, F8).\"],\"confidence\":70,\"pushback\":\"That an overflow-only AI will capture the missed calls that matter most. Lunch and staff-busy gaps may not be covered, so measure timing before paying.\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":1455,"promptTokenCount":6968,"thoughtsTokenCount":662,"totalTokenCount":8423},"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"industry_expert_r2","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"devils_advocate","their_claim":"Missed new-patient calls are mostly due to no one answering, so find out how many are lost and when before paying for full coverage.","response":"Agreed on measuring first. A dental office's missed calls often cluster at lunch and during peak hours, not only after hours, so the pilot should log time of day too (F7). Overflow-only coverage may not reach those gaps."},{"target":"strategist","their_claim":"A signed annual contract could lock the practice in beyond the pilot if exit terms are never tested.","response":"Right, and this matters more than the price. Before any pilot, get a month-to-month or pilot-length term in writing. Avoid annual prepayment until the keep-or-drop results are in (F7, F10)."}],"concessions":["Missed-call share and timing are unmeasured, so overflow-only coverage may not fix the real gap.","Annual contract lock-in is a real risk; exit terms should be negotiated before signing."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Conditional yes: pilot after-hours and overflow calls before any full switch.","summary":"Don't sign a full AI replacement this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls while staff keep the main line. Log missed calls by time of day so you know where the gap is. Require a signed BAA, Maryland consent capture, a Dentrix booking test and written pricing with a short exit term before any full switch.","key_points":["Missed new-patient calls are the real problem, and a narrow pilot measures them cheaply (F7).","Pilot pass/fail measures should include transfers to staff and booking errors on real appointment types (F5, F7).","Budget fit and contract exit terms are unconfirmed; get written quotes first (F10)."],"risks":["Wrong bookings in Dentrix on real appointment types, such as new-patient exams and emergencies (F5).","Recording without all-party consent carries felony exposure in Maryland; have an attorney confirm the disclosure script (F1).","Bot-to-staff handoff failures could land on the one front-desk person (F6, F8)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"That an overflow-only AI will capture the missed calls that matter most. Lunch and staff-busy gaps may not be covered, so measure timing before paying."},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/industry_expert_r2@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/industry_expert_r2@1"]},"branch":"industry_expert_r2@1","id":"987b9fec-d353-42f0-86a4-0a0a8bdd9a39","timestamp":1794351886.0613577}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"changed_position\":false,\"changed_because\":\"\",\"rebuttals\":[{\"target\":\"ai_tools_expert\",\"their_claim\":\"Test a second tool, Weave, for missed-call texting alongside the RingCentral pilot.\",\"response\":\"Not yet. Two untested tools split the pilot and blur results. Test one vendor on after-hours calls first, then decide whether a second tool adds value. Weave's booking and Dentrix support are unverified.\"},{\"target\":\"realist\",\"their_claim\":\"Maryland consent and a signed BAA must be in place before any call is recorded or patient data flows.\",\"response\":\"Agreed, and that applies to the pilot too. A pilot on live calls still records and touches patient data, so the consent and BAA gate covers it from day one, not only the full switch.\"}],\"concessions\":[\"A pilot on live calls still records and touches patient data, so consent and BAA gating applies from day one (Realist).\",\"Overflow-only coverage may miss lunch-hour new-patient calls, so the missed-call timing check must come before any scope decision (Devil's Advocate).\"],\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Conditional: pilot after-hours calls; sign no annual contract until tested.\",\"summary\":\"Don't sign this month. A two-week after-hours pilot with agreed pass/fail rules tests the claim cheaply (F7). Before any patient data flows or any call is recorded, get a signed BAA on the exact plan and Maryland consent capture on every call, including transfers (F1, F2, F4). If the pilot passes, the next step is a Dentrix booking test on your real appointment types (F5).\",\"key_points\":[\"A short pilot shows real value before any multi-year lock-in (F7).\",\"Contract exit terms are the real lock-in; get them in writing before any signature (unverified; confirm with an attorney).\",\"Next move after a pass: test Dentrix booking on real appointment types, including new-patient and emergency slots (F5).\"],\"risks\":[\"An annual contract locks you in if exit terms are missed (unverified).\",\"The one front-desk person absorbs bot-to-staff handoff failures (F6).\",\"Patients with clinical concerns who reach a bot could be harmed or delayed (F8).\"],\"confidence\":70,\"pushback\":\"That a pilot can be signed without an annual commitment. Check whether the vendor allows a monthly exit before you agree to any pilot terms.\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":2109,"promptTokenCount":6966,"thoughtsTokenCount":1325,"totalTokenCount":9075},"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"strategist_r2","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"ai_tools_expert","their_claim":"Test a second tool, Weave, for missed-call texting alongside the RingCentral pilot.","response":"Not yet. Two untested tools split the pilot and blur results. Test one vendor on after-hours calls first, then decide whether a second tool adds value. Weave's booking and Dentrix support are unverified."},{"target":"realist","their_claim":"Maryland consent and a signed BAA must be in place before any call is recorded or patient data flows.","response":"Agreed, and that applies to the pilot too. A pilot on live calls still records and touches patient data, so the consent and BAA gate covers it from day one, not only the full switch."}],"concessions":["A pilot on live calls still records and touches patient data, so consent and BAA gating applies from day one (Realist).","Overflow-only coverage may miss lunch-hour new-patient calls, so the missed-call timing check must come before any scope decision (Devil's Advocate)."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Conditional: pilot after-hours calls; sign no annual contract until tested.","summary":"Don't sign this month. A two-week after-hours pilot with agreed pass/fail rules tests the claim cheaply (F7). Before any patient data flows or any call is recorded, get a signed BAA on the exact plan and Maryland consent capture on every call, including transfers (F1, F2, F4). If the pilot passes, the next step is a Dentrix booking test on your real appointment types (F5).","key_points":["A short pilot shows real value before any multi-year lock-in (F7).","Contract exit terms are the real lock-in; get them in writing before any signature (unverified; confirm with an attorney).","Next move after a pass: test Dentrix booking on real appointment types, including new-patient and emergency slots (F5)."],"risks":["An annual contract locks you in if exit terms are missed (unverified).","The one front-desk person absorbs bot-to-staff handoff failures (F6).","Patients with clinical concerns who reach a bot could be harmed or delayed (F8)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"That a pilot can be signed without an annual commitment. Check whether the vendor allows a monthly exit before you agree to any pilot terms."},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/strategist_r2@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/strategist_r2@1"]},"branch":"strategist_r2@1","id":"e40158c7-5ea8-40e2-b120-58dbf7824ae2","timestamp":1794351886.0655284}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"changed_position\": false, \"changed_because\": \"\", \"rebuttals\": [{\"target\": \"devils_advocate\", \"their_claim\": \"The key assumption is that missed new-patient calls mostly happen because nobody answers, so the true loss needs measuring first.\", \"response\": \"Agreed on measuring first. My pilot already tracks missed-call timing (F7). If most misses fall at lunch, overflow-only coverage will not fix them, so I would cut the pilot.\"}, {\"target\": \"strategist\", \"their_claim\": \"A signed annual contract could lock the practice in beyond the pilot.\", \"response\": \"Right, and it is a real risk. Require month-to-month terms or written exit rights before any signature, and confirm the terms with an attorney (unverified).\"}, {\"target\": \"industry_expert\", \"their_claim\": \"Hard calls, complaints and booking fixes still need a human, so a full switch does not free front-desk time.\", \"response\": \"I accept this. The bot should only take after-hours and overflow calls, and clinical, billing and complaint calls must always reach a person (F8).\"}], \"concessions\": [\"Hard clinical, billing and complaint calls must always route to a human (F8).\", \"An annual contract could lock the practice in past the pilot; exit terms must be checked first.\", \"The missed-call problem is unmeasured, so overflow-only coverage may not be enough.\"], \"stance\": \"conditional\", \"headline\": \"Conditional: pilot after-hours and overflow calls; no full switch yet\", \"summary\": \"Do not sign a full AI receptionist this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff keeping the main line. Before any patient data flows, get a signed BAA on the purchased plan and Maryland all-party recording consent (F1, F2, F4). Price is unconfirmed against the $400 budget (F10). Keep only if the pilot proves new-patient bookings.\", \"key_points\": [\"Maryland all-party consent and a BAA on the exact purchased plan are must-haves before any recording or patient data (F1, F2, F4).\", \"A two-week overflow-only pilot with agreed keep-or-drop numbers tests value at low risk (F7).\", \"Prices and Dentrix booking fit are unverified, so get written quotes and test real appointment types (F5, F10, F11).\"], \"risks\": [\"Bookings that do not match real appointment types could double-book chairs (F5).\", \"Handoffs from bot to the one front-desk person usually fail first, which could drop urgent calls (F6, F8).\", \"An annual contract could lock in costs past the pilot (unverified).\"], \"confidence\": 68, \"pushback\": \"Assumes the missed calls are mostly new-patient calls an AI can book. Measure how many are missed and when before paying for any coverage.\", \"tools\": [{\"name\": \"RingCentral AI Receptionist\", \"use\": \"Adds an AI answering layer to the phone line the practice already uses, limited to after-hours and overflow calls.\", \"monthly_cost\": \"unknown; check RingCentral pricing (F11)\", \"works_with\": \"Existing RingCentral phone line; Dentrix link unverified.\", \"caveat\": \"Price and Dentrix fit are unverified (F11); the BAA and consent flow must be confirmed in writing before use.\", \"url\": \"https://www.ringcentral.com\", \"price_fact\": \"\", \"pilot_test\": \"Keep it if after-hours calls yield at least 5 new-patient bookings with under 20% transferred to staff over 2 weeks; cancel before the first invoice otherwise.\"}, {\"name\": \"Weave\", \"use\": \"Dental phone and messaging platform that can text back missed callers and handle basic scheduling.\", \"monthly_cost\": \"$0-400/mo (estimate, unverified)\", \"works_with\": \"Dentrix link unverified; standalone unless confirmed.\", \"caveat\": \"AI booking depth and exact Dentrix support are unverified; confirm BAA and consent handling first.\", \"url\": \"\", \"price_fact\": \"\", \"pilot_test\": \"Keep it if missed-call texts convert at least 5 extra new-patient bookings in 4 weeks; decide before the second invoice.\"}]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":2295,"promptTokenCount":7997,"thoughtsTokenCount":916,"totalTokenCount":10292},"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"ai_tools_expert_r2","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"devils_advocate","their_claim":"The key assumption is that missed new-patient calls mostly happen because nobody answers, so the true loss needs measuring first.","response":"Agreed on measuring first. My pilot already tracks missed-call timing (F7). If most misses fall at lunch, overflow-only coverage will not fix them, so I would cut the pilot."},{"target":"strategist","their_claim":"A signed annual contract could lock the practice in beyond the pilot.","response":"Right, and it is a real risk. Require month-to-month terms or written exit rights before any signature, and confirm the terms with an attorney (unverified)."},{"target":"industry_expert","their_claim":"Hard calls, complaints and booking fixes still need a human, so a full switch does not free front-desk time.","response":"I accept this. The bot should only take after-hours and overflow calls, and clinical, billing and complaint calls must always reach a person (F8)."}],"concessions":["Hard clinical, billing and complaint calls must always route to a human (F8).","An annual contract could lock the practice in past the pilot; exit terms must be checked first.","The missed-call problem is unmeasured, so overflow-only coverage may not be enough."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Conditional: pilot after-hours and overflow calls; no full switch yet","summary":"Do not sign a full AI receptionist this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff keeping the main line. Before any patient data flows, get a signed BAA on the purchased plan and Maryland all-party recording consent (F1, F2, F4). Price is unconfirmed against the $400 budget (F10). Keep only if the pilot proves new-patient bookings.","key_points":["Maryland all-party consent and a BAA on the exact purchased plan are must-haves before any recording or patient data (F1, F2, F4).","A two-week overflow-only pilot with agreed keep-or-drop numbers tests value at low risk (F7).","Prices and Dentrix booking fit are unverified, so get written quotes and test real appointment types (F5, F10, F11)."],"risks":["Bookings that do not match real appointment types could double-book chairs (F5).","Handoffs from bot to the one front-desk person usually fail first, which could drop urgent calls (F6, F8).","An annual contract could lock in costs past the pilot (unverified)."],"confidence":68,"pushback":"Assumes the missed calls are mostly new-patient calls an AI can book. Measure how many are missed and when before paying for any coverage.","tools":[{"name":"RingCentral AI Receptionist","use":"Adds an AI answering layer to the phone line the practice already uses, limited to after-hours and overflow calls.","monthly_cost":"unknown; check RingCentral pricing (F11)","works_with":"Existing RingCentral phone line; Dentrix link unverified.","caveat":"Price and Dentrix fit are unverified (F11); the BAA and consent flow must be confirmed in writing before use.","url":"https://www.ringcentral.com","price_fact":"","pilot_test":"Keep it if after-hours calls yield at least 5 new-patient bookings with under 20% transferred to staff over 2 weeks; cancel before the first invoice otherwise."},{"name":"Weave","use":"Dental phone and messaging platform that can text back missed callers and handle basic scheduling.","monthly_cost":"$0-400/mo (estimate, unverified)","works_with":"Dentrix link unverified; standalone unless confirmed.","caveat":"AI booking depth and exact Dentrix support are unverified; confirm BAA and consent handling first.","url":"","price_fact":"","pilot_test":"Keep it if missed-call texts convert at least 5 extra new-patient bookings in 4 weeks; decide before the second invoice."}]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/ai_tools_expert_r2@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/ai_tools_expert_r2@1"]},"branch":"ai_tools_expert_r2@1","id":"3b2997eb-bd7b-4d27-94dc-669bd1f461d2","timestamp":1794351886.0626912}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"changed_position\":false,\"changed_because\":\"\",\"rebuttals\":[{\"target\":\"ai_tools_expert\",\"their_claim\":\"Pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, keeping the bot only if it books at least 5 new-patient appointments in two weeks.\",\"response\":\"Five bookings from after-hours calls proves little if most misses happen at lunch or peak times. Set the pass test on the calls you actually lose, or the pilot grades the wrong thing (open questions).\"},{\"target\":\"industry_expert\",\"their_claim\":\"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls first, with staff keeping the main line.\",\"response\":\"Staff keeping the main line means the one front-desk person still fields the busy calls. The pilot also needs consent scripts, a BAA and Dentrix testing first (F1, F2, F5), so it is not the low-effort test you describe.\"},{\"target\":\"realist\",\"their_claim\":\"Missed-call share and timing are missing, so the pilot should test whether AI captures new-patient calls.\",\"response\":\"Agreed the data is missing, but the fix is quick: pull RingCentral call logs before any vendor pilot. Deciding scope without that data is guessing, and a pilot does not replace measuring the baseline (open questions).\"}],\"concessions\":[\"A two-week overflow pilot commits far less money than a full annual replacement (F7).\",\"Consent and a signed BAA must be settled before any patient data flows (F1, F2).\"],\"stance\":\"oppose\",\"headline\":\"Pull call logs before any pilot; overflow-only may test the wrong calls\",\"summary\":\"The board's pilot-first plan still front-loads legal and integration work. Maryland consent, a BAA and Dentrix booking must be ready before any real call is handled, so the pilot is not cheap. It covers only after-hours and overflow calls, which may not be where new-patient calls are lost. Pull your RingCentral call logs first.\",\"key_points\":[\"The pilot needs the same consent, BAA and Dentrix work as a full rollout before one call is tested (F1, F2, F5).\",\"Overflow-only coverage helps only when staff are busy; lunch and peak-time misses may fall outside it (open questions).\",\"Pass rules agreed with the vendor may reward easy after-hours bookings, not the real new-patient gap (F7).\"],\"risks\":[\"Recording with no verified Maryland consent script creates felony exposure during the pilot itself (F1).\",\"Bot-to-staff handoffs on overflow calls could drop the very patients the pilot is meant to win (F6).\",\"Pilot setup and staff time could consume the $400 budget before any full decision is made (F10).\"],\"confidence\":66,\"pushback\":\"That overflow calls are where the lost new-patient revenue sits; pull call logs before choosing any pilot scope.\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":5043,"promptTokenCount":7028,"thoughtsTokenCount":4126,"totalTokenCount":12071},"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"devils_advocate_r2","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"ai_tools_expert","their_claim":"Pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, keeping the bot only if it books at least 5 new-patient appointments in two weeks.","response":"Five bookings from after-hours calls proves little if most misses happen at lunch or peak times. Set the pass test on the calls you actually lose, or the pilot grades the wrong thing (open questions)."},{"target":"industry_expert","their_claim":"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls first, with staff keeping the main line.","response":"Staff keeping the main line means the one front-desk person still fields the busy calls. The pilot also needs consent scripts, a BAA and Dentrix testing first (F1, F2, F5), so it is not the low-effort test you describe."},{"target":"realist","their_claim":"Missed-call share and timing are missing, so the pilot should test whether AI captures new-patient calls.","response":"Agreed the data is missing, but the fix is quick: pull RingCentral call logs before any vendor pilot. Deciding scope without that data is guessing, and a pilot does not replace measuring the baseline (open questions)."}],"concessions":["A two-week overflow pilot commits far less money than a full annual replacement (F7).","Consent and a signed BAA must be settled before any patient data flows (F1, F2)."],"stance":"oppose","headline":"Pull call logs before any pilot; overflow-only may test the wrong calls","summary":"The board's pilot-first plan still front-loads legal and integration work. Maryland consent, a BAA and Dentrix booking must be ready before any real call is handled, so the pilot is not cheap. It covers only after-hours and overflow calls, which may not be where new-patient calls are lost. Pull your RingCentral call logs first.","key_points":["The pilot needs the same consent, BAA and Dentrix work as a full rollout before one call is tested (F1, F2, F5).","Overflow-only coverage helps only when staff are busy; lunch and peak-time misses may fall outside it (open questions).","Pass rules agreed with the vendor may reward easy after-hours bookings, not the real new-patient gap (F7)."],"risks":["Recording with no verified Maryland consent script creates felony exposure during the pilot itself (F1).","Bot-to-staff handoffs on overflow calls could drop the very patients the pilot is meant to win (F6).","Pilot setup and staff time could consume the $400 budget before any full decision is made (F10)."],"confidence":66,"pushback":"That overflow calls are where the lost new-patient revenue sits; pull call logs before choosing any pilot scope."},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/devils_advocate_r2@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/devils_advocate_r2@1"]},"branch":"devils_advocate_r2@1","id":"a1335b1e-4ab8-4a08-9559-f8e1163c8eb0","timestamp":1794351886.066466}

data: {"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"devils_advocate_r2":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"ai_tools_expert","their_claim":"Pilot on after-hours and overflow calls, keeping the bot only if it books at least 5 new-patient appointments in two weeks.","response":"Five bookings from after-hours calls proves little if most misses happen at lunch or peak times. Set the pass test on the calls you actually lose, or the pilot grades the wrong thing (open questions)."},{"target":"industry_expert","their_claim":"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls first, with staff keeping the main line.","response":"Staff keeping the main line means the one front-desk person still fields the busy calls. The pilot also needs consent scripts, a BAA and Dentrix testing first (F1, F2, F5), so it is not the low-effort test you describe."},{"target":"realist","their_claim":"Missed-call share and timing are missing, so the pilot should test whether AI captures new-patient calls.","response":"Agreed the data is missing, but the fix is quick: pull RingCentral call logs before any vendor pilot. Deciding scope without that data is guessing, and a pilot does not replace measuring the baseline (open questions)."}],"concessions":["A two-week overflow pilot commits far less money than a full annual replacement (F7).","Consent and a signed BAA must be settled before any patient data flows (F1, F2)."],"stance":"oppose","headline":"Pull call logs before any pilot; overflow-only may test the wrong calls","summary":"The board's pilot-first plan still front-loads legal and integration work. Maryland consent, a BAA and Dentrix booking must be ready before any real call is handled, so the pilot is not cheap. It covers only after-hours and overflow calls, which may not be where new-patient calls are lost. Pull your RingCentral call logs first.","key_points":["The pilot needs the same consent, BAA and Dentrix work as a full rollout before one call is tested (F1, F2, F5).","Overflow-only coverage helps only when staff are busy; lunch and peak-time misses may fall outside it (open questions).","Pass rules agreed with the vendor may reward easy after-hours bookings, not the real new-patient gap (F7)."],"risks":["Recording with no verified Maryland consent script creates felony exposure during the pilot itself (F1).","Bot-to-staff handoffs on overflow calls could drop the very patients the pilot is meant to win (F6).","Pilot setup and staff time could consume the $400 budget before any full decision is made (F10)."],"confidence":66,"pushback":"That overflow calls are where the lost new-patient revenue sits; pull call logs before choosing any pilot scope."},"realist_r2":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"ai_tools_expert","their_claim":"Weave could add missed-call texting and basic scheduling as a lower-cost option.","response":"Weave's $0-400 price is an unverified estimate (unverified). Its BAA, recording consent and Dentrix support must be confirmed in writing before it is treated as a cheap alternative. Any pilot must still capture Maryland consent (F1)."},{"target":"strategist","their_claim":"A signed annual contract could lock the practice in beyond the pilot.","response":"Agreed on the lock-in risk. Require month-to-month terms or a written exit clause before any pilot starts, and have an attorney review the contract (unverified)."},{"target":"devils_advocate","their_claim":"Missed new-patient calls may be mostly due to no one answering, so overflow-only may be enough.","response":"The data is missing. Without the share and timing of missed calls (open questions), overflow-only coverage may miss lunch-hour losses. Measure first, as F7 suggests."}],"concessions":["Overflow-only coverage may not fix lunch-hour or staff-hour misses; the pilot must measure timing, not just volume.","Weave is a plausible alternative to test, not only a full AI receptionist."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Pilot after-hours and overflow calls only, after BAA and consent are confirmed.","summary":"Do not sign a full AI receptionist this month. Maryland requires every caller's consent before recording (F1), and a signed BAA must cover the exact plan bought (F2, F4). Neither the price against your $400 budget nor the Dentrix fit is confirmed (F10, F5). A two-week after-hours and overflow pilot, with agreed keep-or-drop rules, tests the idea cheaply (F7).","key_points":["Legal gate: Maryland all-party consent and a signed BAA on the purchased plan must be in writing first (F1, F2, F4).","Money gate: get total monthly cost with overage and contract term; no vendor price is confirmed against $400 (F10, F11).","Operations gate: test Dentrix booking on your real appointment types and map the human handoff (F5, F6, F8)."],"risks":["Recording without every caller's consent can be a felony in Maryland (F1).","Wrong bookings in Dentrix could double-book or misfill chairs (F5).","Urgent clinical or 'ask for a person' calls trapped in a bot (F8)."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assume missed new-patient calls are mostly unanswered calls that an AI can book. Measure how many are missed and when before paying for any coverage (open questions, F9).","checks":[{"area":"legal","status":"block","note":"Maryland all-party consent (F1) and a signed BAA on the exact plan (F2, F4) must exist before recording or patient data flows; have a healthcare attorney confirm, and check any Montgomery County or Maryland Board of Dental Examiners rules (unverified)."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"Require encryption, sub-processor list, no-training terms, retention limits and MFA on all logins (F3, F4); confirm with the vendor and your IT provider (unverified)."},{"area":"financial","status":"flag","note":"No vendor price is confirmed against $400/month (F10); RingCentral's add-on price is unverified (F11), so get written quotes including overage and contract term."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"Overflow and after-hours coverage targets missed new-patient calls (F7), but only if missed-call data confirms the gap."},{"area":"operational","status":"flag","note":"Dentrix integration and real appointment-type testing are unverified (F5); the single front-desk employee needs a clear handoff plan (F6, F8)."}]},"industry_expert_r2":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"devils_advocate","their_claim":"Missed new-patient calls are mostly due to no one answering, so find out how many are lost and when before paying for full coverage.","response":"Agreed on measuring first. A dental office's missed calls often cluster at lunch and during peak hours, not only after hours, so the pilot should log time of day too (F7). Overflow-only coverage may not reach those gaps."},{"target":"strategist","their_claim":"A signed annual contract could lock the practice in beyond the pilot if exit terms are never tested.","response":"Right, and this matters more than the price. Before any pilot, get a month-to-month or pilot-length term in writing. Avoid annual prepayment until the keep-or-drop results are in (F7, F10)."}],"concessions":["Missed-call share and timing are unmeasured, so overflow-only coverage may not fix the real gap.","Annual contract lock-in is a real risk; exit terms should be negotiated before signing."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Conditional yes: pilot after-hours and overflow calls before any full switch.","summary":"Don't sign a full AI replacement this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls while staff keep the main line. Log missed calls by time of day so you know where the gap is. Require a signed BAA, Maryland consent capture, a Dentrix booking test and written pricing with a short exit term before any full switch.","key_points":["Missed new-patient calls are the real problem, and a narrow pilot measures them cheaply (F7).","Pilot pass/fail measures should include transfers to staff and booking errors on real appointment types (F5, F7).","Budget fit and contract exit terms are unconfirmed; get written quotes first (F10)."],"risks":["Wrong bookings in Dentrix on real appointment types, such as new-patient exams and emergencies (F5).","Recording without all-party consent carries felony exposure in Maryland; have an attorney confirm the disclosure script (F1).","Bot-to-staff handoff failures could land on the one front-desk person (F6, F8)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"That an overflow-only AI will capture the missed calls that matter most. Lunch and staff-busy gaps may not be covered, so measure timing before paying."},"ai_tools_expert_r2":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"devils_advocate","their_claim":"The key assumption is that missed new-patient calls mostly happen because nobody answers, so the true loss needs measuring first.","response":"Agreed on measuring first. My pilot already tracks missed-call timing (F7). If most misses fall at lunch, overflow-only coverage will not fix them, so I would cut the pilot."},{"target":"strategist","their_claim":"A signed annual contract could lock the practice in beyond the pilot.","response":"Right, and it is a real risk. Require month-to-month terms or written exit rights before any signature, and confirm the terms with an attorney (unverified)."},{"target":"industry_expert","their_claim":"Hard calls, complaints and booking fixes still need a human, so a full switch does not free front-desk time.","response":"I accept this. The bot should only take after-hours and overflow calls, and clinical, billing and complaint calls must always reach a person (F8)."}],"concessions":["Hard clinical, billing and complaint calls must always route to a human (F8).","An annual contract could lock the practice in past the pilot; exit terms must be checked first.","The missed-call problem is unmeasured, so overflow-only coverage may not be enough."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Conditional: pilot after-hours and overflow calls; no full switch yet","summary":"Do not sign a full AI receptionist this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls only, with staff keeping the main line. Before any patient data flows, get a signed BAA on the purchased plan and Maryland all-party recording consent (F1, F2, F4). Price is unconfirmed against the $400 budget (F10). Keep only if the pilot proves new-patient bookings.","key_points":["Maryland all-party consent and a BAA on the exact purchased plan are must-haves before any recording or patient data (F1, F2, F4).","A two-week overflow-only pilot with agreed keep-or-drop numbers tests value at low risk (F7).","Prices and Dentrix booking fit are unverified, so get written quotes and test real appointment types (F5, F10, F11)."],"risks":["Bookings that do not match real appointment types could double-book chairs (F5).","Handoffs from bot to the one front-desk person usually fail first, which could drop urgent calls (F6, F8).","An annual contract could lock in costs past the pilot (unverified)."],"confidence":68,"pushback":"Assumes the missed calls are mostly new-patient calls an AI can book. Measure how many are missed and when before paying for any coverage.","tools":[{"name":"RingCentral AI Receptionist","use":"Adds an AI answering layer to the phone line the practice already uses, limited to after-hours and overflow calls.","monthly_cost":"unknown; check RingCentral pricing (F11)","works_with":"Existing RingCentral phone line; Dentrix link unverified.","caveat":"Price and Dentrix fit are unverified (F11); the BAA and consent flow must be confirmed in writing before use.","url":"https://www.ringcentral.com","price_fact":"","pilot_test":"Keep it if after-hours calls yield at least 5 new-patient bookings with under 20% transferred to staff over 2 weeks; cancel before the first invoice otherwise."},{"name":"Weave","use":"Dental phone and messaging platform that can text back missed callers and handle basic scheduling.","monthly_cost":"$0-400/mo (estimate, unverified)","works_with":"Dentrix link unverified; standalone unless confirmed.","caveat":"AI booking depth and exact Dentrix support are unverified; confirm BAA and consent handling first.","url":"","price_fact":"","pilot_test":"Keep it if missed-call texts convert at least 5 extra new-patient bookings in 4 weeks; decide before the second invoice."}]},"strategist_r2":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"ai_tools_expert","their_claim":"Test a second tool, Weave, for missed-call texting alongside the RingCentral pilot.","response":"Not yet. Two untested tools split the pilot and blur results. Test one vendor on after-hours calls first, then decide whether a second tool adds value. Weave's booking and Dentrix support are unverified."},{"target":"realist","their_claim":"Maryland consent and a signed BAA must be in place before any call is recorded or patient data flows.","response":"Agreed, and that applies to the pilot too. A pilot on live calls still records and touches patient data, so the consent and BAA gate covers it from day one, not only the full switch."}],"concessions":["A pilot on live calls still records and touches patient data, so consent and BAA gating applies from day one (Realist).","Overflow-only coverage may miss lunch-hour new-patient calls, so the missed-call timing check must come before any scope decision (Devil's Advocate)."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Conditional: pilot after-hours calls; sign no annual contract until tested.","summary":"Don't sign this month. A two-week after-hours pilot with agreed pass/fail rules tests the claim cheaply (F7). Before any patient data flows or any call is recorded, get a signed BAA on the exact plan and Maryland consent capture on every call, including transfers (F1, F2, F4). If the pilot passes, the next step is a Dentrix booking test on your real appointment types (F5).","key_points":["A short pilot shows real value before any multi-year lock-in (F7).","Contract exit terms are the real lock-in; get them in writing before any signature (unverified; confirm with an attorney).","Next move after a pass: test Dentrix booking on real appointment types, including new-patient and emergency slots (F5)."],"risks":["An annual contract locks you in if exit terms are missed (unverified).","The one front-desk person absorbs bot-to-staff handoff failures (F6).","Patients with clinical concerns who reach a bot could be harmed or delayed (F8)."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"That a pilot can be signed without an annual commitment. Check whether the vendor allows a monthly exit before you agree to any pilot terms."}},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/round2@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/round2@1"]},"id":"8eac9d72-a076-4246-b49c-826e19948fff","timestamp":1794351907.5931492}

data: {"invocationId":"e-76bbcbf4-a906-41ff-8834-c84973a1bc09","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{"round2":{"industry_expert":{"changed_position":false,"changed_because":"","rebuttals":[{"target":"devils_advocate","their_claim":"Missed new-patient calls are mostly due to no one answering, so find out how many are lost and when before paying for full coverage.","response":"Agreed on measuring first. A dental office's missed calls often cluster at lunch and during peak hours, not only after hours, so the pilot should log time of day too (F7). Overflow-only coverage may not reach those gaps."},{"target":"strategist","their_claim":"A signed annual contract could lock the practice in beyond the pilot if exit terms are never tested.","response":"Right, and this matters more than the price. Before any pilot, get a month-to-month or pilot-length term in writing. Avoid annual prepayment until the keep-or-drop results are in (F7, F10)."}],"concessions":["Missed-call share and timing are unmeasured, so overflow-only coverage may not fix the real gap.","Annual contract lock-in is a real risk; exit terms should be negotiated before signing."],"stance":"conditional","headline":"Conditional yes: pilot after-hours and overflow calls before any full switch.","summary":"Don't sign a full AI replacement this month. Run a two-week pilot on after-hours and overflow calls while staff keep the main line. Log missed calls by time of day so you know where the gap is. Require a signed BAA, Maryland consent capture, a Dentrix booking test and written pricing with a short exit term before any full switch.","key_points":["Missed new-patient calls are the real problem, and a narrow pilot measures them cheaply (F7).","Pilot pass/fail measures should include transfers to staff and booking errors on real appointment types (F5, F7).","Budget fit and contract exit terms are unconfirmed; get written quotes first (F10)."],"risks":["Wrong bookings in Dentrix on real appointment types, such as 